window.INTELLECTUALITY_PROFESSOR={"T001":{"id":"T001","topic":"Spinal cord I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Spinal cord I and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["The spinal cord extends from the foramen magnum to roughly L1-L2 in adults; below it, lumbar and sacral roots descend as the cauda equina around the filum terminale.","Gray matter forms dorsal sensory horns, ventral motor horns, and a lateral autonomic horn most prominent in T1-L2. White matter is arranged into posterior, lateral, and anterior funiculi.","A spinal nerve is formed when dorsal sensory and ventral motor roots unite. The dorsal root ganglion contains pseudounipolar sensory neuron cell bodies."],"mental":"Cross-section first: posterior = sensory entry, anterior = motor exit, lateral = autonomic/interneuronal territory.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw one spinal cord cross-section and label dorsal/ventral horns, dorsal/ventral roots, DRG, posterior/lateral/anterior funiculi.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Where is the dorsal root ganglion?","On the dorsal root, containing primary sensory neuron cell bodies."],["Why can a lesion of the ventral root cause weakness without sensory loss?","It interrupts efferent motor axons while sparing the dorsal sensory root."]],"seed":null},"T002":{"id":"T002","topic":"Spinal cord II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Spinal cord II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Long tracts are organized by modality and destination. Posterior columns carry vibration, discriminative touch and conscious proprioception; the anterolateral system carries pain, temperature and crude touch; corticospinal tracts carry voluntary motor commands.","A tract question is solved by four coordinates: modality, first synapse, crossing point, and final destination.","Somatotopy matters: posterior column fibers for lower limb are medial (gracile) and upper limb more lateral (cuneate)."],"mental":"Never memorize a tract as a paragraph. Trace: receptor → neuron 1 → crossing → neuron 2 → thalamus/target → cortex.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Trace dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways side-by-side on one page.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Which modalities use the dorsal columns?","Fine/discriminative touch, vibration and conscious proprioception."],["Why does spinal cord hemisection produce different sides of sensory loss?","Major sensory systems decussate at different levels."]],"seed":null},"T003":{"id":"T003","topic":"Introduction to synapse","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Introduction to synapse as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["A synapse converts an electrical event in the presynaptic neuron into chemical release, then back into an electrical postsynaptic response. Action-potential depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels; Ca2+ triggers vesicle fusion and transmitter release.","Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated channels and act quickly. Metabotropic receptors use G proteins/second messengers and are slower but modulatory.","Excitation and inhibition are not properties of the transmitter alone; they depend on the receptor and ion conductance produced."],"mental":"AP → Ca2+ → vesicle → receptor → postsynaptic conductance.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw the five-step chemical synapse and label Ca2+, vesicle, cleft, receptor, reuptake/degradation.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["What directly triggers neurotransmitter release?","Presynaptic Ca2+ entry through voltage-gated calcium channels."],["Why can one transmitter have different effects?","Different receptor subtypes couple to different ion channels or second-messenger pathways."]],"seed":null},"T004":{"id":"T004","topic":"Neurons and synapses","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Recognize Neurons and synapses visually, name its discriminating features, and separate it from its nearest look-alikes.","teach":["Neurons have a soma, dendritic receptive region and axon output region. Nissl substance represents rough ER and is absent from the axon hillock/axon.","Multipolar neurons dominate motor/interneuronal circuits; pseudounipolar neurons are typical sensory ganglion cells; bipolar neurons occur in special sensory pathways.","A synapse may be axodendritic, axosomatic or axoaxonic; the last is especially useful for presynaptic modulation."],"mental":"Histology asks: what cell, what process, what stainable feature, what function?","imaginePrompt":"Zoom from low power to high power: overall architecture → signature cell/layer → discriminator → function. Compare against the nearest confusing image.","draw":"Sketch a multipolar neuron and mark soma, Nissl, axon hillock, dendrites, axon.","examGrammar":"Expect identify → discriminator/features → function/nearest look-alike. Train visual discrimination, not prose recall.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify from architecture, then state 2 discriminating microscopic features without relying on labels.","checks":[["Where is Nissl substance absent?","Axon hillock and axon."],["Typical cell shape in dorsal root ganglion?","Pseudounipolar neuron."]],"seed":null},"T005":{"id":"T005","topic":"PNS nerves, ganglia, degeneration and regeneration","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Understand peripheral nerve structure, ganglia, degeneration and regeneration as a single histologic story.","teach":["A peripheral nerve is a bundle of axons supported by Schwann cells and three connective-tissue sheaths. Endoneurium surrounds individual nerve fibers, perineurium encloses fascicles and forms an important diffusion barrier, and epineurium surrounds the whole nerve and carries larger vessels.","Myelinated PNS axons are wrapped by Schwann cells. One Schwann cell myelinates one internode of one axon. In unmyelinated fibers, one Schwann cell can cradle several axons without forming compact myelin.","Sensory ganglia contain large pseudounipolar neurons surrounded by a conspicuous ring of satellite cells. Autonomic ganglia contain multipolar neurons, more irregularly distributed satellite cells and true synapses within the ganglion.","After axonal transection, the distal segment undergoes Wallerian degeneration. Myelin and axonal debris are removed by Schwann cells and macrophages, while the proximal neuron may show chromatolysis.","Regeneration is possible when the neuronal cell body survives and the connective-tissue pathway remains usable. Schwann cells proliferate into Bands of Büngner that guide sprouts toward the distal target. Poor alignment or a large gap can produce failed reinnervation or a traumatic neuroma."],"mental":"Think nerve fiber → fascicle → whole nerve, then injury → cleanup → Schwann-cell tunnel → regrowth.","imaginePrompt":"Zoom from a single axon outward through endoneurium, perineurium and epineurium, then cut the axon and watch the distal segment disappear while Schwann cells form a guidance tube.","draw":"Draw a nerve fascicle with the three sheaths, then a before/after Wallerian degeneration sketch.","examGrammar":"Expect sheath identification, sensory-vs-autonomic ganglion discrimination, Schwann-vs-oligodendrocyte contrasts and conditions that permit regeneration.","practicalCue":"On a nerve cross-section, identify fascicles and perineurium first. In a ganglion, decide sensory versus autonomic from neuron shape, arrangement and satellite cells.","checks":[["Which sheath surrounds a fascicle?","Perineurium."],["What is the key cellular guide for PNS axonal regrowth?","Schwann cells forming Bands of Büngner."]],"seed":null},"T006":{"id":"T006","topic":"Synaptic potentials and transmission","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Synaptic potentials and transmission as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["EPSPs are graded depolarizations that increase firing probability; IPSPs usually hyperpolarize or shunt the membrane. Neither is an all-or-none action potential.","Temporal summation adds repeated inputs from one synapse; spatial summation adds simultaneous inputs from multiple synapses.","Presynaptic inhibition often occurs at axoaxonic synapses and reduces transmitter release without necessarily changing the postsynaptic membrane directly."],"mental":"The axon hillock is a vote counter: EPSPs and IPSPs sum until threshold is or is not reached.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Graph two subthreshold EPSPs summating to threshold and contrast with an IPSP.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Difference between temporal and spatial summation?","Temporal sums inputs over time from a source; spatial sums inputs from different locations."],["How can presynaptic inhibition reduce output?","By decreasing transmitter release from the presynaptic terminal, often via reduced Ca2+ entry."]],"seed":null},"T007":{"id":"T007","topic":"Brainstem I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Brainstem I and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["The brainstem is midbrain, pons and medulla. It contains ascending/descending tracts, cranial nerve nuclei, reticular formation and vital autonomic centers.","A brainstem cross-section becomes manageable if you first identify level, then the ventricular landmark, then cranial nerve nuclei, then long tracts.","Medulla contains pyramids and olives; pons has a bulky basilar part and middle cerebellar peduncles; midbrain has cerebral peduncles and tectum."],"mental":"Level → ventricle/aqueduct → CN nuclei → tracts → cerebellar connections.","imaginePrompt":"Picture a stacked midbrain–pons–medulla column. Place motor structures ventrally, sensory systems dorsolaterally, then pin cranial nuclei and long tracts onto the stack.","draw":"Make a three-row brainstem map: midbrain, pons, medulla; add one landmark and cranial nerves for each.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["What are the three brainstem divisions?","Midbrain, pons and medulla."],["Which surface landmark is characteristic of the medulla?","Pyramids and olives on the ventral surface."]],"seed":null},"T008":{"id":"T008","topic":"Brainstem II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Brainstem II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Cranial nerve nuclei are organized into longitudinal functional columns. Motor nuclei lie relatively medial and sensory nuclei more lateral because the fourth ventricle opens the dorsal brainstem.","Long-tract lesions plus ipsilateral cranial nerve findings create classic crossed brainstem syndromes: cranial nerve deficit on the side of the lesion, body tract deficit on the opposite side depending on tract crossing.","The medial longitudinal fasciculus coordinates conjugate gaze by linking vestibular and ocular motor nuclei."],"mental":"Crossed face/body signs scream brainstem localization.","imaginePrompt":"Picture a stacked midbrain–pons–medulla column. Place motor structures ventrally, sensory systems dorsolaterally, then pin cranial nuclei and long tracts onto the stack.","draw":"Draw a simplified open-medulla/pons floor with motor medial, sensory lateral.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Why are sensory nuclei more lateral in much of brainstem?","The opening of the roof into the fourth ventricle displaces alar-plate sensory derivatives laterally."],["What tract coordinates eye movements between ocular motor nuclei?","Medial longitudinal fasciculus."]],"seed":null},"T009":{"id":"T009","topic":"Sensory receptors and afferents","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Sensory receptors and afferents as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Sensory receptors transduce stimulus energy into receptor potentials. Adequate stimulus is the modality to which a receptor is most sensitive.","Rapidly adapting receptors emphasize change and vibration; slowly adapting receptors signal maintained stimulus and static information.","Receptive-field size and lateral inhibition determine spatial discrimination; smaller fields and stronger lateral inhibition improve localization."],"mental":"Stimulus → receptor potential → afferent firing code → CNS interpretation.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Compare a rapidly adapting and slowly adapting receptor response to a sustained stimulus.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["What does adaptation describe?","Change in receptor/afferent response during a sustained stimulus."],["Why do fingertips localize touch well?","Small receptive fields and high receptor density/lateral inhibition."]],"seed":null},"T010":{"id":"T010","topic":"Cerebellum and fourth ventricle","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Cerebellum and fourth ventricle and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["The cerebellum consists of vermis plus hemispheres and communicates through superior, middle and inferior cerebellar peduncles. Deep nuclei from lateral to medial are dentate, emboliform, globose and fastigial.","Functional divisions: vestibulocerebellum for equilibrium/eye movements, spinocerebellum for posture and ongoing movement, cerebrocerebellum for planning and skilled movement.","The fourth ventricle lies between pons/upper medulla anteriorly and cerebellum posteriorly, continuing rostrally with the cerebral aqueduct and caudally with the central canal."],"mental":"Cerebellum compares intended vs actual movement and corrects error rather than initiating strength.","imaginePrompt":"Imagine the ventricular system as connected hollow rooms. Walk CSF through each doorway before attaching boundary lists to the walls.","draw":"Draw cerebellar lobes, peduncles and the fourth ventricle relationship to pons/medulla.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Name the three cerebellar peduncles.","Superior, middle and inferior."],["What type of deficit is typical of cerebellar lesions?","Ipsilateral coordination/ataxia-type deficits rather than primary paralysis."]],"seed":null},"T011":{"id":"T011","topic":"Neuroglia and nerve endings","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Build a visual-and-functional map of neuroglia and the major sensory nerve endings.","teach":["Astrocytes are CNS homeostatic support cells. Protoplasmic astrocytes predominate in gray matter, fibrous astrocytes in white matter. Their end-feet help regulate the extracellular environment and contribute to the blood-brain barrier interface.","Oligodendrocytes form CNS myelin. A single oligodendrocyte can myelinate portions of multiple axons, unlike a Schwann cell, which myelinates one internode of one PNS axon.","Microglia are the resident immune/phagocytic cells of the CNS. They are small cells with elongated nuclei and ramified processes when surveying tissue, and become more amoeboid when activated.","Ependymal cells line ventricles and the central canal. Specialized ependymal cells participate in the choroid plexus, where CSF is produced by a blood-CSF barrier system.","Peripheral sensory endings range from free nerve endings for pain/temperature to encapsulated receptors. Pacinian corpuscles are lamellated and detect vibration/rapid pressure changes; Meissner corpuscles are superficial touch receptors; muscle spindles detect muscle length; Golgi tendon organs detect tension."],"mental":"For every glial cell ask location + morphology + job. For every nerve ending ask stimulus + location + capsule/architecture.","imaginePrompt":"Picture a CNS neighborhood: astrocyte touching vessels, oligodendrocyte wrapping several axons, microglia patrolling, ependyma lining a ventricle.","draw":"Make a 2-column CNS/PNS glia table and sketch a Pacinian corpuscle beside a muscle spindle.","examGrammar":"The examiner likes nearest-neighbor swaps: astrocyte vs oligodendrocyte, Schwann vs satellite cell, Pacinian vs Meissner, spindle vs tendon organ.","practicalCue":"Use architecture first: concentric onion-like lamellae strongly suggest Pacinian corpuscle; sensory ganglia show satellite-cell rings.","checks":[["Which CNS glial cell forms myelin?","Oligodendrocyte."],["Which receptor detects muscle length/stretch?","Muscle spindle."]],"seed":null},"T012":{"id":"T012","topic":"Diencephalon and third ventricle","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Diencephalon and third ventricle and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["The diencephalon surrounds the third ventricle and includes thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus and subthalamus. The thalamus is the major relay/integration station for most sensory information reaching cortex except olfaction.","The hypothalamus forms much of the floor/inferolateral wall of the third ventricle and coordinates autonomic, endocrine, temperature, hunger/thirst and circadian functions.","The third ventricle communicates with each lateral ventricle through the interventricular foramen and with the fourth ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct."],"mental":"Thalamus routes information; hypothalamus regulates homeostasis.","imaginePrompt":"Imagine the ventricular system as connected hollow rooms. Walk CSF through each doorway before attaching boundary lists to the walls.","draw":"Draw a midsagittal diencephalon with third ventricle, thalamus, hypothalamus, pituitary connection and aqueduct.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Which sensory system largely bypasses the thalamus before primary cortex?","Olfaction."],["Third to fourth ventricle connection?","Cerebral aqueduct."]],"seed":null},"T013":{"id":"T013","topic":"Cerebral hemispheres: surfaces, sulci and gyri","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Cerebral hemispheres surfaces and base and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Use the central sulcus and lateral sulcus as anchors. Precentral gyrus is primary motor cortex; postcentral gyrus is primary somatosensory cortex. The calcarine sulcus borders primary visual cortex medially in the occipital lobe.","The inferior surface relates to orbit and middle cranial fossa; medial surface exposes corpus callosum, cingulate region, paracentral lobule and medial occipital landmarks.","Homuncular organization places lower limb more medial and face more inferolateral on motor/sensory cortices."],"mental":"Anchor every cortical map with central sulcus, lateral sulcus and corpus callosum.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw lateral and medial hemispheric surfaces and label central sulcus, pre/postcentral gyri, paracentral lobule, calcarine sulcus.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Primary motor cortex location?","Precentral gyrus."],["Why does ACA stroke preferentially affect leg?","Medial motor/sensory cortex for lower limb is supplied mainly by ACA."]],"seed":null},"T014":{"id":"T014","topic":"Sensory pathways: introduction and cortex","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Understand how a stimulus becomes a conscious cortical sensation and how the cortex organizes that information.","teach":["A sensory pathway starts with a receptor transducing a stimulus into electrical activity. The first-order neuron carries that signal into the CNS, where it synapses and joins an ascending pathway.","Conscious somatic sensation reaches the thalamus before the cerebral cortex. The thalamus is not just a wire: it relays and filters information, while the primary somatosensory cortex creates an ordered body map.","The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus. It is somatotopically organized, so neighboring body regions are represented in neighboring cortical regions, but representation size reflects sensory resolution rather than physical body size.","Two major body pathways dominate exam logic: dorsal column–medial lemniscus for fine touch, vibration and conscious proprioception, and the anterolateral system for pain, temperature and crude touch.","Localization depends on where each pathway crosses. A lesion before decussation and a lesion after decussation can produce opposite-sided deficits despite damaging the same modality."],"mental":"Modality → receptor → first-order neuron → first synapse → crossing → thalamus → cortical map.","imaginePrompt":"Run one touch signal from a fingertip to the postcentral gyrus and mark the exact point where its pathway crosses.","draw":"Draw a generic three-neuron conscious sensory pathway and then annotate where DCML and spinothalamic pathways differ.","examGrammar":"Questions rotate modality, crossing level, lesion side and cortical territory. Solve the path, do not memorize a sentence.","practicalCue":"On a lateral brain view, identify the central sulcus and postcentral gyrus before assigning sensory function.","checks":[["Where is primary somatosensory cortex?","Postcentral gyrus."],["Why does crossing level matter clinically?","It determines the side of deficit produced by a lesion at a given level."]],"seed":null},"T015":{"id":"T015","topic":"Cerebral hemispheres: medial and inferior surfaces and base","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Map the medial and inferior cerebral surfaces so sulci, gyri and functional cortex become one navigable surface rather than disconnected labels.","teach":["On the medial surface, the corpus callosum is the major landmark. The cingulate gyrus arches above it and continues posteriorly toward the parahippocampal region.","The parieto-occipital sulcus and calcarine sulcus define key occipital territories. Primary visual cortex lies along the banks of the calcarine sulcus.","The paracentral lobule surrounds the medial continuation of the central sulcus and contains lower-limb motor and sensory representation.","On the inferior surface, the frontal orbital surface lies anteriorly, while the temporal-occipital surface contains the parahippocampal and occipitotemporal regions. The uncus is the anterior medial projection of the parahippocampal gyrus.","Questions rotate the same map: identify a gyrus from its sulcal borders, infer function from cortical location, or reverse a relation from structure→neighbor to neighbor→structure."],"mental":"Medial brain = callosal arch + cingulate ring + paracentral lobule + visual calcarine map; inferior brain adds orbital and parahippocampal landmarks.","imaginePrompt":"Rotate the hemisphere from lateral to medial, find the corpus callosum, then trace cingulate → paracentral → parieto-occipital → calcarine → parahippocampal landmarks.","draw":"Sketch the medial hemisphere and label corpus callosum, cingulate gyrus, paracentral lobule, parieto-occipital sulcus, calcarine sulcus and parahippocampal gyrus.","examGrammar":"Expect sulcus↔gyrus relations, functional cortex localization, medial lower-limb representation and inferior-surface identification.","practicalCue":"Orient anterior/posterior, identify the corpus callosum, then use calcarine/parieto-occipital sulci to lock the medial surface.","checks":[["Where is primary visual cortex located?","Along the banks of the calcarine sulcus."],["Which medial lobule carries much of the lower-limb motor/sensory representation?","Paracentral lobule."]],"seed":null},"T016":{"id":"T016","topic":"Conscious sensation from the body","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Master conscious body sensation by comparing DCML and anterolateral pathways rather than memorizing them separately.","teach":["The dorsal column–medial lemniscus pathway carries vibration, discriminative touch and conscious proprioception. First-order fibers ascend ipsilaterally in the spinal cord and synapse in gracile or cuneate nuclei in the medulla.","Second-order DCML neurons cross in the medulla as internal arcuate fibers, ascend as the medial lemniscus and reach the VPL nucleus of the thalamus. Third-order neurons project to primary somatosensory cortex.","The anterolateral/spinothalamic system carries pain, temperature and crude touch. First-order neurons synapse soon after entering the spinal cord.","Second-order spinothalamic neurons cross within the spinal cord through the anterior white commissure and then ascend contralaterally toward the thalamus.","That crossing difference creates classic lesion logic: a spinal hemicord can cause ipsilateral loss of dorsal-column modalities below the lesion but contralateral loss of pain/temperature beginning a little below it."],"mental":"DCML climbs first and crosses later; spinothalamic synapses and crosses early.","imaginePrompt":"Picture two colored lines entering the same side of the cord. One climbs ipsilaterally to the medulla; the other crosses almost immediately.","draw":"Trace DCML and spinothalamic pathways side-by-side from receptor to cortex.","examGrammar":"Expect modality swaps, crossing-site swaps, gracile/cuneate discrimination and hemicord side-of-loss questions.","practicalCue":"On cord sections, orient posterior columns versus anterolateral white matter before naming a tract.","checks":[["Where does DCML decussate?","Medulla, after synapse in gracile/cuneate nuclei."],["Where does spinothalamic decussation occur?","In the spinal cord via the anterior white commissure."]],"seed":null},"T017":{"id":"T017","topic":"Sensory code, mechanoreception and thermal sensation","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Conscious sensation, sensory code, mechanoreception, thermal as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Sensory coding uses labeled lines, firing frequency, recruitment and temporal patterns to represent modality and intensity. Location is encoded by receptive fields and topographic maps.","Mechanoreceptors respond to deformation; thermoreceptors are free nerve endings whose channels change activity with temperature.","Perception is a cortical interpretation of patterned afferent input, not a property located inside the receptor itself."],"mental":"The CNS reads which line fired, how fast, how many fibers and when.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Create a 4-box sensory code: modality, intensity, location, duration.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["How is stimulus intensity commonly encoded?","Higher firing frequency and recruitment of additional afferents."],["What determines modality in labeled-line coding?","The activated receptor/pathway and its central destination."]],"seed":null},"T018":{"id":"T018","topic":"Unconscious sensation and short tracts","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Unconscious sensation and short tracts as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Unconscious proprioception tells the cerebellum what the limbs and trunk are actually doing while movement is happening. Muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs and joint receptors continuously report length, tension and position, but this stream is used mainly for coordination rather than conscious perception.","For the lower limb and lower trunk, posterior spinocerebellar fibers arise mainly from Clarke's column after primary afferents enter the cord. They ascend ipsilaterally and enter the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle, preserving a largely same-side body map.","The anterior spinocerebellar tract is different: spinal interneuronal activity is sampled, many fibers cross in the cord, ascend, then cross again on entering the cerebellum through the superior cerebellar peduncle. The double crossing helps explain why its net functional representation is still mainly ipsilateral.","Upper-limb unconscious proprioception uses the cuneocerebellar route: primary afferents ascend in the fasciculus cuneatus to the accessory cuneate nucleus, then project to the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle.","Short propriospinal fibers stay within or near the spinal cord and connect neighboring segments. They help coordinate multisegmental reflexes and distribute local information up and down the cord without sending it to cortex.","The exam distinction is simple: dorsal columns are for conscious proprioception and fine touch to cortex; spinocerebellar pathways are for unconscious proprioceptive feedback to cerebellum."],"mental":"Unconscious proprioception is feedback for movement correction, not conscious perception.","imaginePrompt":"Imagine walking with your eyes closed. The cortex decides to move, but the cerebellum needs a live hidden feed reporting limb position every fraction of a second so it can correct errors before you notice them.","draw":"Trace one spinocerebellar route from receptor to cerebellum.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"On a spinal-cord pathway diagram, identify whether the target is cortex or cerebellum, then use crossing pattern and peduncle to separate dorsal-column from spinocerebellar routes.","checks":[["Primary role of spinocerebellar pathways?","Unconscious proprioceptive feedback for coordination."],["Why do cerebellar deficits tend to be ipsilateral?","Cerebellar connections often remain ipsilateral or double-cross."]],"seed":null},"T019":{"id":"T019","topic":"Pain I","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Pain I as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Nociceptors are free nerve endings activated by damaging mechanical, thermal or chemical stimuli.","Fast pain is carried mainly by thinly myelinated A-delta fibers; slow aching/burning pain mainly by unmyelinated C fibers.","Second-order pain fibers cross in the spinal cord through the anterior white commissure and ascend in the anterolateral system."],"mental":"Pain pathway: nociceptor → DRG → dorsal horn → cross in cord → anterolateral tract → thalamus/cortex.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Trace fast pain from skin to cortex.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Where do pain fibers cross in spinal cord?","Near entry level via anterior white commissure."],["Fiber type for slow pain?","C fibers."]],"seed":null},"T020":{"id":"T020","topic":"Association and commissural fibres","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Association and commissural fibres and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Association fibers connect cortical areas within the same hemisphere; short U-fibers link adjacent gyri and long bundles link distant lobes.","Commissural fibers connect the two hemispheres; the corpus callosum is the major commissure.","Damage causes disconnection syndromes rather than simple peripheral weakness."],"mental":"Association = same side; commissural = across midline.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw corpus callosum and two long association fasciculi schematically.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Major cerebral commissure?","Corpus callosum."],["What do U-fibers connect?","Adjacent cortical gyri within one hemisphere."]],"seed":null},"T021":{"id":"T021","topic":"Projection fibres and basal nuclei","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Projection fibres and basal nuclei and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Projection fibers connect cortex with subcortical structures, brainstem and spinal cord; many converge through the internal capsule.","The internal capsule is strategically important because compact motor and sensory fibers pass through it.","Basal nuclei modulate movement selection through cortical-subcortical loops rather than directly innervating lower motor neurons."],"mental":"Internal capsule is the traffic bottleneck between cortex and the rest of the neuraxis.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw corona radiata converging into internal capsule then cerebral peduncle.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Why can a small internal capsule lesion cause dense deficits?","Many compact motor and sensory projection fibers are packed together."],["Do basal nuclei directly activate skeletal muscle?","No, they modulate cortical motor circuits."]],"seed":null},"T022":{"id":"T022","topic":"Pain II and referred pain","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Pain II and referred pain as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Visceral and somatic afferents can converge on the same second-order spinal neurons, so the brain may mislocalize visceral pain to a somatic territory: referred pain.","Pain has sensory-discriminative and affective components, involving thalamocortical and limbic/reticular networks.","Hyperalgesia and sensitization reflect increased responsiveness in peripheral and/or central nociceptive circuits."],"mental":"Referred pain = convergence plus the brain's prior map of somatic input.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Sketch visceral + somatic convergence onto one dorsal-horn neuron.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Mechanistic basis of referred pain?","Convergence of visceral and somatic afferents on common central neurons."],["Why is pain more than a location signal?","It also recruits affective/arousal networks."]],"seed":null},"T023":{"id":"T023","topic":"Sensory pathways from body, face and cerebellum","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Integrate body, face and cerebellar sensory routes so you can predict destination and lesion effects.","teach":["Conscious sensation from the body reaches the VPL thalamus, while much conscious somatic sensation from the face travels through trigeminal pathways to the VPM thalamus.","Facial touch and proprioceptive information is processed through trigeminal sensory nuclei; pain and temperature descend within the trigeminal system before crossing and ascending toward the thalamus.","Unconscious proprioceptive information is routed toward the cerebellum rather than the sensory cortex. Spinocerebellar pathways provide the cerebellum with information about ongoing movement and body position.","The cerebellum compares intended movement with sensory feedback and adjusts timing, force, posture and coordination. Because cerebellar connections often cross twice or remain functionally ipsilateral, hemisphere lesions classically produce ipsilateral limb incoordination.","Do not mix destination with modality: two pathways can carry proprioceptive information but serve different purposes, conscious perception versus online coordination."],"mental":"Conscious body → VPL → cortex; conscious face → VPM → cortex; unconscious proprioception → cerebellum.","imaginePrompt":"Route three signals at once: vibration from a foot, pain from a cheek and unconscious proprioception from a leg. Send each to the correct relay and destination.","draw":"Build a three-lane map: body conscious, face conscious, cerebellar unconscious.","examGrammar":"The examiner can rotate body versus face, VPL versus VPM, conscious versus unconscious proprioception and ipsilateral cerebellar signs.","practicalCue":"When looking at brainstem/cerebellar anatomy, identify the cerebellar peduncles as gateways for these inputs and outputs.","checks":[["Which thalamic nucleus relays body somatic sensation?","VPL."],["Why are cerebellar limb signs often ipsilateral?","Cerebellar motor influence is functionally double-crossed or ipsilateral."]],"seed":null},"T024":{"id":"T024","topic":"Motor tracts: pyramidal and extrapyramidal","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Understand descending motor control as pathways with origins, crossings, targets and characteristic lesion signs.","teach":["The corticospinal system is the main descending pathway for skilled voluntary movement, especially fractionated distal-limb movements. Fibers arise mainly from motor and premotor cortex, converge through the corona radiata and posterior limb of the internal capsule, then descend through cerebral peduncle, pons and medullary pyramids.","About most corticospinal fibers cross in the caudal medulla at the pyramidal decussation and descend as the lateral corticospinal tract. A smaller anterior corticospinal component stays more ventromedial and mainly influences axial/proximal musculature, often through bilateral segmental connections.","A lesion above the pyramidal decussation produces weakness predominantly on the opposite side of the body; a spinal-cord corticospinal lesion below the decussation produces weakness predominantly on the same side below the lesion. Crossing level turns laterality questions into geometry.","Upper motor neuron lesions remove descending control over spinal motor circuits. After the acute phase, the classic pattern is weakness with increased tone, hyperreflexia, clonus and extensor plantar response, with relatively little early denervation atrophy.","Lower motor neurons are the final common pathway from anterior horn cells or cranial motor nuclei to skeletal muscle. Their lesions cause weakness with reduced tone and reflexes, denervation atrophy and often fasciculations.","Extrapyramidal is not one tract. Vestibulospinal and reticulospinal pathways strongly influence posture, extensor tone and automatic adjustments; rubrospinal influence is comparatively smaller in humans. Think of them as parallel brainstem control systems that tune the spinal motor apparatus around the corticospinal command."],"mental":"Cortex → internal capsule → brainstem → pyramidal crossing → spinal LMN → muscle, with parallel brainstem systems tuning posture and tone.","imaginePrompt":"Send a command from motor cortex through a narrow internal-capsule bottleneck, watch it cross in the medulla, then decide which side of the body loses power if you cut the pathway at three different levels.","draw":"Draw corticospinal descent and add a UMN-versus-LMN lesion table.","examGrammar":"Expect crossing-side logic, internal-capsule lesions, UMN/LMN contrasts and pathway-function matching.","practicalCue":"On a pathway diagram or lesion vignette, locate the lesion relative to the pyramidal decussation first, then predict side and UMN/LMN pattern.","checks":[["Where do most corticospinal fibers cross?","Pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla."],["Give two LMN signs.","Reduced tone/reflexes and denervation atrophy or fasciculations."]],"seed":null},"T025":{"id":"T025","topic":"Headache and pain control","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Headache and pain control as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Descending analgesic pathways can suppress nociceptive transmission in the dorsal horn through brainstem circuits and endogenous opioids.","Periaqueductal gray, medullary nuclei and dorsal-horn interneurons form a classic descending pain-control network.","Headache mechanisms vary; meningeal/vascular and trigeminal nociceptive pathways are clinically important."],"mental":"Pain is modulated at multiple levels; descending control can turn down dorsal-horn transmission.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw PAG → medulla → dorsal horn inhibition.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Key midbrain region in descending analgesia?","Periaqueductal gray."],["What can endogenous opioids do?","Reduce nociceptive neurotransmission centrally."]],"seed":null},"T026":{"id":"T026","topic":"Lateral ventricle, meninges and CSF","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Lateral ventricle, meninges and CSF and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Lateral ventricles communicate with the third ventricle through interventricular foramina; CSF then passes aqueduct → fourth ventricle → subarachnoid space.","Meninges are dura, arachnoid and pia. CSF circulates mainly in ventricles/subarachnoid space and is absorbed via arachnoid granulations into venous sinuses.","Obstruction of CSF flow can cause noncommunicating hydrocephalus; impaired absorption can cause communicating hydrocephalus."],"mental":"CSF route is a plumbing diagram: lateral → III → aqueduct → IV → apertures → subarachnoid → arachnoid granulations.","imaginePrompt":"Imagine the ventricular system as connected hollow rooms. Walk CSF through each doorway before attaching boundary lists to the walls.","draw":"Draw the CSF circulation loop.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Third to fourth ventricle connection?","Cerebral aqueduct."],["Main CSF reabsorption site?","Arachnoid granulations into dural venous sinuses."]],"seed":null},"T027":{"id":"T027","topic":"Lesions and localization","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Turn neurologic deficits into an anatomic location before thinking about disease names.","teach":["Localization begins by describing the deficit precisely: motor, sensory, coordination, cranial nerve, autonomic or cortical. Then ask whether the pattern is focal, multifocal, diffuse or length-dependent.","Central lesions often combine long-tract signs, cortical signs or cranial-nerve-plus-body patterns. Peripheral lesions follow roots, plexuses, individual nerves, neuromuscular junctions or muscles.","Side is generated by crossing anatomy. If a tract has already crossed before the lesion, the deficit will be contralateral to its original body side; if it has not yet crossed, the deficit can remain ipsilateral.","Brainstem lesions are famous for crossed findings: an ipsilateral cranial nerve deficit with a contralateral body deficit can point to a specific level.","Localization should precede etiology. 'Where is the lesion?' is a different question from 'What disease caused it?' and solving the first dramatically narrows the second."],"mental":"Deficit type → central/peripheral → side → level → tract/nucleus/nerve → only then disease.","imaginePrompt":"Given right facial weakness plus left body pain loss, picture the brainstem and ask which structures can be hit together.","draw":"Create a localization decision tree from symptom pattern to cortex, brainstem, cord, root, nerve, NMJ or muscle.","examGrammar":"The examiner supplies a deficit bundle and expects the smallest anatomical site that explains all of it.","practicalCue":"Use visible landmarks to anchor levels before attaching syndromes. Never jump to a named syndrome without the anatomy.","checks":[["What comes first, localization or disease naming?","Localization."],["What pattern strongly suggests a brainstem lesion?","Ipsilateral cranial-nerve findings with contralateral body findings."]],"seed":null},"T028":{"id":"T028","topic":"Cerebrum, meninges and blood-brain barrier","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Recognize cerebral cortex, meninges and the blood-brain barrier histologically and link each structure to function.","teach":["Neocortex is organized into six layers whose cellular composition changes with function. Pyramidal neurons are especially prominent in motor cortex, while granular layers are more developed in sensory cortex.","White matter beneath cortex contains myelinated axons and glia rather than cortical neuronal layers. This gray-white transition is a reliable orientation clue.","The meninges are dura, arachnoid and pia. Arachnoid trabeculae span the CSF-filled subarachnoid space, while pia closely follows the surface of the CNS.","The blood-brain barrier is produced primarily by tight junctions between CNS capillary endothelial cells, supported by basement membrane, pericytes and astrocytic end-feet. Astrocytes support the barrier but do not form the endothelial tight junctions themselves.","Choroid plexus is different: specialized epithelial/ependymal cells with tight junctions form the blood-CSF barrier around a vascular core and secrete CSF."],"mental":"Cortex layers sit on white matter; meninges wrap outside; BBB is endothelial; choroid plexus is epithelial blood-CSF barrier.","imaginePrompt":"Move from skull inward through dura, arachnoid, subarachnoid space, pia, cortex and white matter, then zoom into a capillary.","draw":"Sketch six-layer neocortex, the three meninges and a BBB capillary with endothelial tight junctions.","examGrammar":"Nearest traps are BBB endothelium vs astrocytes, pia vs arachnoid, gray vs white matter and motor vs sensory cortical layering.","practicalCue":"Orient a cerebral section by pial surface first, then layers, then white matter.","checks":[["What cell junction creates the BBB seal?","Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells."],["Which meninx closely follows the CNS surface?","Pia mater."]],"seed":null},"T029":{"id":"T029","topic":"Blood supply of the brain I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Blood supply of brain I-II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems supply the brain and connect through the circle of Willis.","ACA supplies much of medial frontal/parietal cortex; MCA supplies much of lateral hemisphere; PCA supplies much of occipital/inferomedial temporal cortex.","Deep perforators, especially lenticulostriate arteries, supply internal structures and are vulnerable in small-vessel disease."],"mental":"ACA = leg/medial; MCA = face-arm-language/lateral; PCA = vision/occipital is the useful first-pass map.","imaginePrompt":"Trace blood from origin → course → named branches → territory. Then remove one branch and predict the deficit/ischemic territory.","draw":"Draw circle of Willis and shade ACA/MCA/PCA cortical territories.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Artery most associated with lateral hemisphere?","Middle cerebral artery."],["Primary visual cortex is mainly supplied by?","Posterior cerebral artery."]],"seed":null},"T030":{"id":"T030","topic":"Blood supply of the brain II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Turn cerebral blood supply into a territory-and-lesion map: major arteries, circle of Willis, deep perforators and the deficits produced when each route fails.","teach":["The anterior circulation comes from the internal carotids and gives the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. The posterior circulation comes from vertebral arteries joining as the basilar artery and then posterior cerebral arteries.","The circle of Willis links anterior and posterior circulations through the anterior communicating and posterior communicating arteries. It is an anastomotic ring, but collateral effectiveness varies between people.","ACA predominantly supplies medial frontal/parietal cortex, MCA most of the lateral hemisphere, and PCA occipital/inferomedial temporal cortex. These territories explain many classic focal deficits.","Deep perforating arteries are small end-artery-like branches supplying structures such as basal ganglia and internal capsule. Their small caliber makes a tiny vascular lesion capable of producing a dense motor or sensory deficit.","Exam questions often reverse the map: artery→territory, territory→artery, lesion→deficit, or named branch→deep structure."],"mental":"Surface territory plus deep perforators: ACA medial, MCA lateral, PCA visual/posterior; tiny internal-capsule perforators can cause huge deficits.","imaginePrompt":"Paint the cerebral cortex with three colors for ACA, MCA and PCA, then drive small perforators inward toward basal ganglia and internal capsule.","draw":"Draw a simple circle of Willis and a lateral/medial cortical territory map for ACA, MCA and PCA.","examGrammar":"Expect artery↔territory reversals, circle-of-Willis connections, deep perforator supply and clinical localization.","practicalCue":"On a brain specimen, orient the base first, identify internal carotid/basilar routes, then trace ACA, MCA and PCA directions.","checks":[["Which artery supplies most of the lateral cerebral hemisphere?","Middle cerebral artery."],["Why can a small deep perforator infarct cause marked weakness?","It may damage densely packed corticospinal fibers in the internal capsule."]],"seed":null},"T031":{"id":"T031","topic":"External and middle ear","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Recognize External and middle ear visually, name its discriminating features, and separate it from its nearest look-alikes.","teach":["External ear conducts sound to the tympanic membrane. The middle ear contains malleus, incus and stapes, which transmit/amplify vibration to the oval window.","The auditory (pharyngotympanic) tube links middle ear with nasopharynx for pressure equalization and is clinically important in spread of infection.","Tensor tympani is innervated by V3; stapedius by facial nerve."],"mental":"Sound route: pinna → canal → tympanic membrane → ossicles → oval window.","imaginePrompt":"Follow a sound/head-motion event through the physical structures until it becomes a CN VIII signal and central representation.","draw":"Draw ossicles in order and the auditory tube to nasopharynx.","examGrammar":"Expect identify → discriminator/features → function/nearest look-alike. Train visual discrimination, not prose recall.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify from architecture, then state 2 discriminating microscopic features without relying on labels.","checks":[["Innervation of stapedius?","Facial nerve."],["Middle ear communicates with nasopharynx through?","Pharyngotympanic/auditory tube."]],"seed":null},"T032":{"id":"T032","topic":"Stretch reflex I","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Stretch reflex I-II as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Muscle spindles detect muscle length and rate of stretch. Ia afferents excite alpha motor neurons supplying the same muscle, producing the monosynaptic stretch reflex.","Gamma motor neurons set spindle sensitivity by contracting intrafusal fiber ends.","Reciprocal inhibition relaxes antagonists through inhibitory interneurons."],"mental":"Spindle asks 'how long/how fast stretching?'; GTO asks 'how much tension?'.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw spindle Ia afferent → alpha motor neuron + inhibitory interneuron to antagonist.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Receptor for stretch reflex?","Muscle spindle."],["What maintains spindle sensitivity during contraction?","Gamma motor neuron activity."]],"seed":{"title":"Stretch reflex I","minimumModel":"A rapid muscle stretch activates muscle-spindle Ia afferents, which enter the cord and monosynaptically excite alpha motor neurons to the homonymous muscle; reciprocal inhibition relaxes the antagonist through an inhibitory interneuron.","confusions":["muscle spindle vs Golgi tendon organ","Ia afferent vs gamma efferent","agonist excitation vs antagonist inhibition"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace receptor → Ia afferent → spinal synapse → alpha motor neuron → muscle, then add reciprocal inhibition.","transferPrompt":"Predict the reflex if the Ia afferent is interrupted.","visualAnchor":"One reflex-arc schematic with spindle, Ia fiber, alpha motor neuron and inhibitory interneuron."}},"T033":{"id":"T033","topic":"Development of the spinal cord","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build spinal-cord embryology from the neural tube outward, then use dorsal/ventral patterning to predict adult anatomy and defects.","teach":["The spinal cord develops from the caudal neural tube. Its lumen persists as the central canal, while the surrounding neuroepithelium generates neurons and glia.","Each side of the neural tube differentiates into an alar plate dorsally and a basal plate ventrally, separated by the sulcus limitans. Alar derivatives are predominantly sensory; basal derivatives are predominantly motor.","Roof and floor plates remain thin midline regions. Growing axons around the gray matter form the future white matter, while neural crest cells outside the cord form dorsal-root ganglia and much of the peripheral nervous system.","Because the vertebral column lengthens faster than the spinal cord, the conus ends progressively higher and lower roots lengthen into the cauda equina.","Exam logic is derivative-based: neural tube versus neural crest, alar versus basal, sensory versus motor, and how differential growth explains adult levels."],"mental":"Neural tube is the central cord; alar is dorsal sensory, basal is ventral motor; neural crest builds the sensory ganglia outside.","imaginePrompt":"Picture a hollow embryonic tube. Split each side into dorsal alar and ventral basal plates, then pull the vertebral column downward faster than the cord to create the cauda equina.","draw":"Draw a transverse neural tube with alar plate, basal plate, sulcus limitans, roof/floor plates and a neural-crest-derived dorsal-root ganglion.","examGrammar":"Expect derivatives, neural tube versus neural crest, alar/basal comparisons, and adult consequences of differential growth.","practicalCue":"On an embryologic diagram, orient dorsal/ventral first, then identify alar, basal and neural crest derivatives.","checks":[["Which plate gives rise predominantly to motor regions?","Basal plate."],["What embryologic population forms dorsal-root ganglia?","Neural crest cells."]],"seed":{"title":"Development of spinal cord","minimumModel":"The spinal cord develops from the caudal neural tube. Alar plates become predominantly sensory dorsal regions, basal plates motor ventral regions, the sulcus limitans separates them, and the lumen persists as the central canal.","confusions":["alar vs basal plate","roof vs floor plate","neural tube vs neural crest"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct alar versus basal plate derivatives and orient dorsal versus ventral.","transferPrompt":"Predict the functional consequence of selective failure of a basal-plate motor population.","visualAnchor":"Transverse embryonic neural-tube section showing alar/basal plates and sulcus limitans."}},"T034":{"id":"T034","topic":"Development of the brain and neural crest","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Development spinal cord, brain and neural crest and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Neural tube forms CNS; neural crest forms many PNS structures including sensory/autonomic ganglia and Schwann cells.","Alar plates are primarily sensory and basal plates primarily motor; sulcus limitans separates them.","Brain develops from primary then secondary vesicles; ventricular spaces are remnants of the neural tube lumen."],"mental":"Neural tube = CNS; neural crest = much of PNS.","imaginePrompt":"Run a time-lapse: primitive structure → fold/migrate/fuse → adult derivative. Stop at each failure point and imagine the resulting anomaly.","draw":"Draw neural tube with alar/basal plates and list neural crest derivatives.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Embryologic source of dorsal root ganglia?","Neural crest."],["Basal plate gives rise mainly to?","Motor nuclei/neurons."]],"seed":{"title":"Development of brain and neural crest","minimumModel":"The rostral neural tube expands into primary then secondary brain vesicles, while neural crest cells delaminate and migrate to form much of the peripheral nervous system and many craniofacial derivatives.","confusions":["neural tube vs neural crest","telencephalon vs diencephalon","metencephalon vs myelencephalon"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map prosencephalon, mesencephalon and rhombencephalon to their major secondary vesicles, then contrast neural-tube and neural-crest fates.","transferPrompt":"Given a peripheral ganglion defect, decide whether neural crest involvement is plausible and justify.","visualAnchor":"Brain-vesicle sequence beside a neural-crest migration schematic."}},"T035":{"id":"T035","topic":"Scalp and muscles of the face","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Scalp and face muscles and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["SCALP layers: skin, connective tissue, aponeurosis, loose areolar tissue, pericranium. The dense connective layer explains profuse bleeding from cut vessels.","Facial expression muscles are supplied by facial nerve; muscles of mastication by mandibular division of trigeminal nerve.","The loose areolar layer is a danger area because infection/blood can spread and emissary veins connect extracranial veins with dural venous sinuses."],"mental":"Face movement = VII; mastication = V3.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Write SCALP mnemonic and sketch facial nerve branches over face.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Motor supply of muscles of facial expression?","Facial nerve."],["Danger layer of scalp?","Loose areolar tissue."]],"seed":null},"T036":{"id":"T036","topic":"Stretch reflex II","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Extend the stretch reflex from its basic arc into gamma control, spindle sensitivity, muscle tone and descending modulation.","teach":["The basic stretch reflex uses muscle-spindle Ia afferents to excite alpha motor neurons of the stretched muscle, but the spindle must remain sensitive even while that muscle actively shortens.","Gamma motor neurons contract the polar ends of intrafusal fibers. This keeps the central sensory region taut, preserving spindle discharge during muscle shortening.","Alpha–gamma coactivation therefore lets extrafusal contraction and spindle sensitivity travel together. Gamma activity changes reflex gain rather than directly generating the main muscle force.","Dynamic spindle responses emphasize rate of change, while static responses emphasize maintained muscle length. Descending pathways can increase or decrease gamma drive and spinal reflex excitability.","This is the bridge to tone and hyperreflexia: loss of normal descending inhibition can leave spinal reflex circuits excessively excitable."],"mental":"Alpha makes force; gamma tunes the sensor. Coactivate them so the spindle stays informative while the muscle shortens.","imaginePrompt":"Shorten an extrafusal muscle. Without gamma drive the spindle would go slack; now tighten intrafusal ends and watch sensory firing remain useful.","draw":"Draw a spindle with intrafusal fibers, Ia ending, alpha motor neuron and gamma motor neuron; then add descending control.","examGrammar":"Expect alpha vs gamma, Ia vs efferent roles, static vs dynamic spindle behavior, and effects of descending facilitation/inhibition.","practicalCue":"If shown a spindle diagram, identify sensory center, contractile polar ends and separate alpha from gamma output.","checks":[["What is the main purpose of gamma motor activity?","To maintain and adjust muscle-spindle sensitivity."],["Does gamma activation directly provide the main force of skeletal-muscle contraction?","No. Alpha motor neurons drive extrafusal force; gamma tunes intrafusal spindle tension."]],"seed":{"title":"Stretch reflex II","minimumModel":"Gamma motor activity adjusts intrafusal fiber tension and therefore spindle sensitivity, allowing the stretch reflex to remain responsive during muscle shortening; descending pathways can increase or suppress reflex gain.","confusions":["alpha vs gamma motor neurons","static vs dynamic spindle responses","reflex gain vs muscle strength"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Explain alpha–gamma coactivation and how it preserves spindle sensitivity during contraction.","transferPrompt":"Predict how excessive descending facilitation can contribute to hyperreflexia or spasticity.","visualAnchor":"Spindle showing intrafusal ends, sensory center, alpha and gamma outputs."}},"T037":{"id":"T037","topic":"Polysynaptic reflexes and cord transection","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Polysynaptic reflexes and cord transection as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Withdrawal reflexes use multiple interneurons to flex a limb away from a noxious stimulus; crossed extensor reflexes stabilize the opposite limb.","Acute severe cord transection can cause spinal shock: transient loss of reflexes and flaccidity below lesion, followed later by return/exaggeration of reflexes as supraspinal influence is lost.","Autonomic and sensory consequences depend on lesion level and completeness."],"mental":"Monosynaptic = stretch; polysynaptic = protective network response.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw withdrawal on one side with crossed extension on the other.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Immediate reflex state below complete cord transection?","Spinal shock with depressed reflexes/flaccidity initially."],["Purpose of crossed extensor reflex?","Support the opposite limb during withdrawal."]],"seed":{"title":"Polysynaptic reflexes and cord transection","minimumModel":"Withdrawal is a polysynaptic, often multisegmental reflex using interneurons to activate flexors and inhibit extensors; crossed extension can stabilize the opposite limb. Acute severe cord transection initially removes descending facilitation and can cause spinal shock before reflex activity later returns.","confusions":["monosynaptic stretch vs polysynaptic withdrawal","ipsilateral withdrawal vs crossed extension","spinal shock vs chronic hyperreflexia"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace withdrawal and crossed-extensor circuitry, then describe the broad time-course after acute cord transection.","transferPrompt":"Predict the immediate versus later reflex state below a complete cord transection.","visualAnchor":"Bilateral spinal-cord reflex schematic with flexor/extensor interneurons."}},"T038":{"id":"T038","topic":"Face: vessels and nerves","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Face vessels and nerves and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Facial artery is tortuous and palpable where it crosses the mandible anterior to masseter; facial vein is relatively valveless and communicates with deeper venous channels.","Sensory innervation of the face is mainly trigeminal divisions V1, V2 and V3; motor facial expression is facial nerve.","The 'danger triangle' matters because facial venous connections can communicate with cavernous sinus pathways."],"mental":"Separate sensation (V) from expression (VII).","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Draw facial artery course and V1/V2/V3 sensory territories.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Main sensory nerve of face?","Trigeminal nerve."],["Main motor nerve for facial expression?","Facial nerve."]],"seed":{"title":"Face vessels and nerves","minimumModel":"The face receives major arterial supply from facial, superficial temporal and maxillary systems; facial sensation is mainly trigeminal while muscles of facial expression are supplied by facial nerve branches. Venous channels communicate with deeper routes, which explains clinically important spread patterns.","confusions":["facial artery vs facial vein","CN V sensory vs CN VII motor","superficial facial vs deep infratemporal vessels"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map facial artery course, major sensory territories and facial-nerve motor branches.","transferPrompt":"Use a sensory deficit plus motor weakness pattern to separate trigeminal from facial-nerve injury.","visualAnchor":"Surface face with arterial course, trigeminal territories and facial-nerve branches."}},"T039":{"id":"T039","topic":"Parotid gland","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Parotid gland and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Parotid encloses facial nerve branches, retromandibular vein and external carotid artery in a clinically important superficial-to-deep arrangement.","Parasympathetic secretomotor supply originates with CN IX, synapses in the otic ganglion and reaches parotid via auriculotemporal nerve.","Parotid duct crosses masseter, turns medially, pierces buccinator and opens opposite the upper second molar."],"mental":"VII passes through parotid but does not provide its secretomotor supply.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw parotid relations and secretomotor route IX → otic → auriculotemporal.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Preganglionic parasympathetic source for parotid?","Glossopharyngeal nerve."],["Where does parotid duct open?","Oral vestibule opposite upper second molar."]],"seed":null},"T040":{"id":"T040","topic":"Inner ear","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Recognize Inner ear visually, name its discriminating features, and separate it from its nearest look-alikes.","teach":["Bony labyrinth contains perilymph; membranous labyrinth contains endolymph. Cochlea serves hearing, while utricle/saccule and semicircular ducts serve linear and angular acceleration.","Hair cells transduce mechanical deflection of stereocilia into receptor potentials.","The vestibular and cochlear divisions join as CN VIII."],"mental":"Cochlea = hearing; semicircular canals = angular acceleration; utricle/saccule = linear/head tilt.","imaginePrompt":"Follow a sound/head-motion event through the physical structures until it becomes a CN VIII signal and central representation.","draw":"Draw bony vs membranous labyrinth and label cochlea, vestibule, semicircular canals.","examGrammar":"Expect identify → discriminator/features → function/nearest look-alike. Train visual discrimination, not prose recall.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify from architecture, then state 2 discriminating microscopic features without relying on labels.","checks":[["Fluid in membranous labyrinth?","Endolymph."],["Receptor organ for hearing?","Organ of Corti in cochlear duct."]],"seed":null},"T041":{"id":"T041","topic":"Brainstem and equilibrium I","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Brainstem and equilibrium I as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Vestibular nuclei integrate labyrinth, visual and proprioceptive information. Outputs reach ocular motor nuclei through MLF, spinal cord through vestibulospinal pathways and cerebellum through vestibulocerebellar connections.","The vestibulo-ocular reflex stabilizes gaze during head movement by producing compensatory eye movements.","Lesions can produce vertigo, nystagmus, imbalance and nausea depending on peripheral vs central site."],"mental":"Balance is a three-sensor vote: vestibular + vision + proprioception.","imaginePrompt":"Picture a stacked midbrain–pons–medulla column. Place motor structures ventrally, sensory systems dorsolaterally, then pin cranial nuclei and long tracts onto the stack.","draw":"Draw vestibular apparatus → vestibular nuclei → eyes/cerebellum/spinal cord.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Tract linking vestibular nuclei with ocular motor nuclei?","Medial longitudinal fasciculus."],["Purpose of VOR?","Stabilize retinal image during head movement."]],"seed":{"title":"Brainstem and equilibrium I","minimumModel":"Vestibular receptors signal head motion and position through CN VIII to vestibular nuclei and cerebellum; these centers integrate visual and proprioceptive input and project to ocular motor nuclei and spinal pathways to stabilize gaze and posture.","confusions":["cochlear vs vestibular division","vestibular nuclei vs cerebellum","vestibulo-ocular vs vestibulospinal output"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace vestibular receptor → vestibular nerve → vestibular nuclei/cerebellum → eye and spinal outputs.","transferPrompt":"Predict why a unilateral vestibular imbalance can cause vertigo, nystagmus and postural deviation.","visualAnchor":"Vestibular apparatus connected to vestibular nuclei, ocular nuclei and spinal cord."}},"T042":{"id":"T042","topic":"Equilibrium II and posture","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Equilibrium II and posture as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Posture reflects tonic integration of vestibular, visual, proprioceptive and reticulospinal influences.","Vestibulospinal pathways facilitate antigravity/extensor adjustments, while reticulospinal systems modulate posture and tone.","Cerebellum calibrates these responses by comparing intended and actual movement."],"mental":"Posture is an automatic motor-control problem, not a conscious sequence.","imaginePrompt":"Follow a sound/head-motion event through the physical structures until it becomes a CN VIII signal and central representation.","draw":"Sketch vestibular/reticular/cerebellar inputs converging on spinal motor circuits.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Which descending system strongly supports extensor postural responses?","Vestibulospinal system."],["What does cerebellum contribute?","Error correction and coordination."]],"seed":{"title":"Equilibrium II and posture","minimumModel":"Postural control combines vestibular, visual and proprioceptive signals with cerebellar and brainstem motor outputs. Semicircular canals emphasize angular acceleration, while utricle and saccule encode linear acceleration and head position relative to gravity.","confusions":["semicircular canals vs otolith organs","static posture vs dynamic gaze stabilization","vestibular vs proprioceptive input"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Compare canal versus otolith signals and explain how brainstem/cerebellar outputs maintain posture.","transferPrompt":"Predict which cues become more important when vision is removed during balance.","visualAnchor":"Head-motion diagram showing canals, utricle/saccule and postural outputs."}},"T043":{"id":"T043","topic":"Muscles of mastication and TMJ","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Muscles of mastication and TMJ and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids are supplied by V3. Elevation closes jaw; lateral pterygoid helps protrude and open by pulling condyle/disc anteriorly.","TMJ is a modified synovial joint with an articular disc dividing the joint into upper translational and lower rotational compartments.","Dislocation typically involves anterior displacement of mandibular condyle."],"mental":"V3 moves the jaw; disc lets TMJ rotate and translate.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw TMJ disc and show rotation vs translation.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Motor innervation of mastication?","Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve."],["Muscle important for protrusion/opening?","Lateral pterygoid."]],"seed":{"title":"Muscles of mastication and TMJ","minimumModel":"Masseter, temporalis and medial pterygoid elevate the mandible; lateral pterygoid helps protrusion and translation. These muscles receive motor supply from V3. The TMJ combines hinge and gliding movements between mandibular condyle, articular disc and temporal bone.","confusions":["facial expression vs mastication","rotation vs translation at TMJ","V3 motor vs V2 sensory"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct each major mastication muscle action and explain the two phases of TMJ opening.","transferPrompt":"Predict jaw deviation after unilateral weakness of muscles supplied by V3.","visualAnchor":"TMJ sagittal diagram plus four mastication muscles."}},"T044":{"id":"T044","topic":"Maxillary nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Maxillary nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["V2 is sensory. It passes through foramen rotundum into pterygopalatine fossa and gives branches to midface, palate, nasal cavity and upper teeth.","Pterygopalatine ganglion receives preganglionic parasympathetic fibers ultimately from facial nerve via greater petrosal nerve; postganglionic fibers distribute with V2 branches.","Sympathetic fibers pass through without synapsing."],"mental":"V2 is the distribution highway; facial parasympathetics hitchhike on it after the ganglion.","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Draw V2 in pterygopalatine fossa and parasympathetic route.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["V2 exits skull via?","Foramen rotundum."],["Pterygopalatine ganglion function?","Parasympathetic relay for lacrimal/nasal/palatal glands."]],"seed":{"title":"Maxillary nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion","minimumModel":"V2 is a sensory division of trigeminal nerve that passes through foramen rotundum into the pterygopalatine fossa. The pterygopalatine ganglion hangs from V2; parasympathetic fibers from the greater petrosal pathway synapse there and then hitchhike on V2 branches to lacrimal, nasal and palatal targets.","confusions":["V2 sensory vs parasympathetic hitchhikers","pterygopalatine vs otic ganglion","greater vs deep petrosal contributions"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace V2 into the pterygopalatine fossa and distinguish sensory fibers from parasympathetic fibers using its branches.","transferPrompt":"Predict the sensory and secretomotor consequences of a lesion within the pterygopalatine fossa.","visualAnchor":"Pterygopalatine fossa map with V2, ganglion and major outgoing branches."}},"T045":{"id":"T045","topic":"Cornea and sclera","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Recognize Cornea and sclera visually, name its discriminating features, and separate it from its nearest look-alikes.","teach":["Cornea is transparent and avascular with highly organized collagen; its epithelium regenerates well but endothelial loss is clinically important.","Sclera is dense opaque collagenous connective tissue continuous anteriorly with cornea and posteriorly with optic-nerve coverings.","Corneal transparency depends on regular stromal organization, relative dehydration and avascularity."],"mental":"Cornea = clear optical window; sclera = tough opaque shell.","imaginePrompt":"Picture light entering the eye, being focused onto retina, converted into neural activity, then crossing at the chiasm toward cortex.","draw":"Draw corneal layers from epithelium to endothelium.","examGrammar":"Expect identify → discriminator/features → function/nearest look-alike. Train visual discrimination, not prose recall.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify from architecture, then state 2 discriminating microscopic features without relying on labels.","checks":[["Why is cornea transparent?","Regular collagen arrangement, avascularity and controlled hydration."],["Is cornea vascular?","No, normally avascular."]],"seed":{"title":"Cornea and sclera histology","minimumModel":"The cornea is transparent and classically organized into epithelium, Bowman layer, stroma, Descemet membrane and endothelium; stromal organization and endothelial fluid control support transparency. The sclera is dense collagenous connective tissue continuous with the cornea at the limbus.","confusions":["corneal epithelium vs endothelium","Bowman vs Descemet membrane","transparent corneal stroma vs opaque scleral collagen"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"List corneal layers from anterior to posterior and give the defining structural contrast with sclera.","transferPrompt":"Given a section at the limbus, distinguish cornea from sclera using visible architecture.","visualAnchor":"Cornea-to-sclera limbus section with five corneal layers."}},"T046":{"id":"T046","topic":"Mandibular nerve and otic ganglion","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Mandibular nerve and otic ganglion and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["V3 is mixed: sensory to lower face/oral structures and motor to muscles of mastication plus mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani and tensor veli palatini.","It exits via foramen ovale. Major branches include auriculotemporal, inferior alveolar, lingual and buccal nerves.","Otic ganglion receives CN IX parasympathetics; postganglionic fibers join auriculotemporal nerve to parotid."],"mental":"V3 = mastication motor + lower-face/oral sensory.","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Draw V3 four major sensory branches and otic ganglion route.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["V3 exits via?","Foramen ovale."],["Otic ganglion secretomotor target?","Parotid gland."]],"seed":{"title":"Mandibular nerve and otic ganglion","minimumModel":"V3 exits through foramen ovale and carries both general sensory fibers and branchial motor fibers to muscles of mastication. The otic ganglion is functionally parasympathetic: preganglionic fibers from CN IX synapse there and postganglionic fibers reach parotid through the auriculotemporal nerve.","confusions":["V3 vs V2","otic vs pterygopalatine ganglion","auriculotemporal sensory carriage vs secretomotor hitchhiking"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map V3 main sensory/motor branches and trace CN IX parasympathetic fibers through the otic ganglion to parotid.","transferPrompt":"Separate deficits from a V3 lesion versus an isolated otic-ganglion parasympathetic lesion.","visualAnchor":"Infratemporal map with V3 divisions, otic ganglion and auriculotemporal nerve."}},"T047":{"id":"T047","topic":"Motor cortex","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Motor cortex as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Primary motor cortex is in precentral gyrus and controls contralateral voluntary movement through corticospinal/corticobulbar outputs.","Premotor and supplementary motor areas contribute to planning/programming of movement.","Motor homunculus places leg medially, face laterally, with disproportionate cortical representation for skilled regions."],"mental":"Motor cortex plans and commands; spinal/brainstem circuits execute final patterns.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw motor homunculus orientation.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Primary motor cortex location?","Precentral gyrus."],["Lower limb motor area is mostly supplied by?","ACA territory medially."]],"seed":{"title":"Motor cortex","minimumModel":"Primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus contains a somatotopic map and contributes major corticospinal/corticobulbar output. Premotor and supplementary regions help plan and sequence movement rather than directly representing simple muscle force.","confusions":["precentral vs postcentral gyrus","primary motor vs premotor cortex","corticospinal vs corticobulbar output"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Draw a simple motor homunculus and distinguish primary motor, premotor and supplementary motor roles.","transferPrompt":"Predict the broad motor pattern of a focal lesion in the medial versus lateral primary motor cortex.","visualAnchor":"Lateral and medial motor homunculus."}},"T048":{"id":"T048","topic":"UMN versus LMN and motor tracts","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain UMN vs LMN and tracts as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["UMN lesions classically produce weakness with increased tone, hyperreflexia and Babinski sign after acute phase; LMN lesions produce flaccidity, reduced reflexes, atrophy and fasciculations.","UMNs descend from cortex/brainstem to synapse on LMNs; LMNs directly innervate skeletal muscle.","Acute UMN injury may initially appear flaccid, so timing matters."],"mental":"UMN removes descending control; LMN cuts the final motor wire.","imaginePrompt":"Mentally travel with the signal: receptor → neuron order → synapse → crossing → destination. Rotate the pathway and ask what a lesion before/after the crossing would do.","draw":"Make a 2-column UMN vs LMN table.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Babinski suggests which level?","Upper motor neuron/corticospinal lesion."],["Fasciculations strongly suggest?","Lower motor neuron involvement."]],"seed":null},"T049":{"id":"T049","topic":"Maxillary artery and pterygoid venous plexus","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Maxillary artery and pterygoid plexus and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Maxillary artery is a terminal branch of external carotid and is divided by its relation to lateral pterygoid into three parts with many deep-face branches.","Middle meningeal artery is a clinically important branch entering foramen spinosum.","Pterygoid venous plexus communicates with facial vein and cavernous sinus region, creating potential routes for spread."],"mental":"Maxillary artery is the deep-face arterial trunk; pterygoid plexus is its venous network companion.","imaginePrompt":"Trace blood from origin → course → named branches → territory. Then remove one branch and predict the deficit/ischemic territory.","draw":"Draw three maxillary-artery parts and middle meningeal branch.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Middle meningeal artery enters skull via?","Foramen spinosum."],["Why is pterygoid plexus clinically relevant?","Venous communications can spread infection toward cavernous sinus."]],"seed":{"title":"Maxillary artery and pterygoid venous plexus","minimumModel":"The maxillary artery is a terminal branch of the external carotid and is conventionally divided into three parts in the infratemporal/pterygopalatine regions; major branches supply deep face, dura, teeth, nasal cavity and palate. The pterygoid venous plexus surrounds the pterygoid region and communicates with facial and intracranial venous channels.","confusions":["maxillary artery vs facial artery","middle meningeal vs accessory meningeal","pterygoid plexus vs cavernous sinus"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct the three-part organization and anchor major branches to compartments rather than memorizing one flat list.","transferPrompt":"Predict a clinically relevant route of venous spread from the face through deep venous communications.","visualAnchor":"Infratemporal/pterygopalatine map with maxillary artery and venous plexus."}},"T050":{"id":"T050","topic":"Cranial cavity I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Cranial cavity I-II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Anterior, middle and posterior cranial fossae support frontal lobes, temporal lobes, and cerebellum/brainstem respectively.","High-yield foramina map: cribriform plate I; optic canal II + ophthalmic artery; superior orbital fissure III/IV/V1/VI; rotundum V2; ovale V3; internal acoustic meatus VII/VIII; jugular foramen IX/X/XI; hypoglossal canal XII.","Dural folds and venous sinuses partition and drain intracranial spaces."],"mental":"Learn skull base by nerve traffic, not by a random hole list.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw three fossae and a foramen→nerve map.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Foramen rotundum carries?","V2."],["Jugular foramen carries?","CN IX, X, XI plus major venous drainage structures."]],"seed":{"title":"Cranial cavity I","minimumModel":"The cranial base is organized into anterior, middle and posterior fossae whose bones, foramina and transmitted neurovascular structures form a reusable map. Learn each foramen as location → contents → nearest neighboring opening.","confusions":["foramen rotundum vs ovale vs spinosum","jugular foramen vs hypoglossal canal","optic canal vs superior orbital fissure"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Draw the three cranial fossae and place major foramina with their principal contents.","transferPrompt":"Given a skull-base lesion at one foramen, predict the cranial nerves or vessels at risk.","visualAnchor":"Internal cranial-base diagram with fossae and key foramina."}},"T051":{"id":"T051","topic":"Basal ganglia physiology","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Basal ganglia physiology as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Direct pathway facilitates selected movement by reducing inhibitory output from GPi/SNr to thalamus; indirect pathway suppresses competing movement.","Dopamine from substantia nigra pars compacta promotes movement overall by exciting D1 direct-pathway neurons and inhibiting D2 indirect-pathway neurons.","Parkinson disease reflects dopaminergic loss; Huntington disease preferentially disrupts indirect-pathway striatal neurons early."],"mental":"Direct = release the brake; indirect = strengthen the brake; dopamine favors movement.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw direct and indirect pathways with inhibitory/excitatory signs.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Net effect of direct pathway?","Facilitates desired movement."],["Dopamine overall effect on movement?","Promotes movement."]],"seed":{"title":"Basal ganglia physiology","minimumModel":"The basal ganglia form cortico-striato-pallido-thalamo-cortical loops that select and scale actions. Simplified direct-pathway activity facilitates selected movement, while indirect-pathway activity suppresses competing movement; dopamine modulates these circuits through D1/D2 effects.","confusions":["caudate/putamen vs globus pallidus","direct vs indirect pathway","movement initiation vs cerebellar coordination"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct cortex → striatum → pallidal output → thalamus → cortex and explain direct versus indirect influence on movement.","transferPrompt":"Use the circuit to explain why dopamine loss biases movement toward hypokinesia.","visualAnchor":"Simplified direct/indirect basal-ganglia loop."}},"T052":{"id":"T052","topic":"Cranial cavity II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Use the cranial cavity as a route map for foramina, dural folds, venous sinuses and the clinically important cavernous-sinus neighborhood.","teach":["The cranial fossae are not just floors. Their foramina are controlled gateways through which cranial nerves and vessels leave or enter the skull.","Dural folds such as falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli partition the cranial cavity and contain dural venous sinuses along selected margins.","Dural sinuses are endothelial-lined venous channels between dural layers. They receive cerebral veins and CSF from arachnoid granulations and ultimately drain toward the internal jugular veins.","The cavernous sinus sits beside the body of the sphenoid and is clinically dense: the internal carotid artery and CN VI run within it, while CN III, IV, V1 and V2 are related to its lateral wall.","Questions commonly rotate foramina and contents, sinus relations and routes by which infection or thrombosis produces multiple cranial-nerve findings."],"mental":"Cranial cavity II = gateways + dural partitions + venous highways, with the cavernous sinus as the high-density crossroads.","imaginePrompt":"Stand inside the middle cranial fossa, locate the sphenoid, then place the cavernous sinus on each side and thread carotid plus cranial nerves through/along it.","draw":"Draw a simplified skull base with major foramina, then a cavernous-sinus cross-section showing ICA, VI, III, IV, V1 and V2.","examGrammar":"Expect foramen↔content, dural fold/sinus relations, cavernous sinus anatomy and clinical spread.","practicalCue":"On a skull base, identify the fossa and foramen before naming the transmitted nerve or vessel.","checks":[["Which cranial nerve runs within the cavernous sinus beside the internal carotid artery?","Abducens nerve (CN VI)."],["What is a major final drainage route from dural venous sinuses?","Internal jugular vein via the sigmoid sinus."]],"seed":{"title":"Cranial cavity II","minimumModel":"Dural folds and venous sinuses partition and drain the cranial cavity. The cavernous sinus is especially important because the internal carotid and several cranial nerves have close relationships within or along its wall.","confusions":["dural fold vs venous sinus","cavernous sinus contents vs lateral-wall nerves","straight vs transverse vs sigmoid sinus"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map falx/tentorium and major dural venous sinuses, then reconstruct cavernous-sinus relationships.","transferPrompt":"Predict which eye-movement or facial-sensory deficits can cluster in a cavernous-sinus process.","visualAnchor":"Dural-fold/sinus map plus cavernous sinus cross-section."}},"T053":{"id":"T053","topic":"Choroid, retina and accessory eye structures","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Build a usable exam model of Choroid, retina and accessory eye structures, not a paragraph memory.","teach":["Think of the globe as three coats. Outside is fibrous sclera/cornea, middle is vascular uvea including choroid, and inside is neural retina. Their different tissue architecture is what lets you identify them on a slide.","The choroid is vascular and pigmented. It nourishes the outer retina and absorbs stray light. The retina is laminated nervous tissue, with photoreceptors toward the pigment epithelium and ganglion-cell axons converging toward the optic nerve.","Rods and cones are the photoreceptors. Rods favor dim-light sensitivity; cones support color and high-acuity vision. Accessory structures such as eyelid, conjunctiva and lacrimal gland protect and lubricate the ocular surface."],"mental":"Eye histology is structure serving optics: transparent media, photoreceptors, pigment and focusing tissues.","imaginePrompt":"Zoom through the wall of the eye from sclera inward: pigmented vascular choroid, then laminated retina, then follow ganglion-cell axons toward the optic nerve. Now reverse direction and trace light toward the photoreceptors.","draw":"Draw cornea layers, lens and retina as a simplified optical stack.","examGrammar":"Identify tissue/feature and connect it to transparency, refraction or phototransduction.","practicalCue":"On an eye slide, identify sclera by dense collagen, choroid by pigment/vascularity and retina by its layered neural architecture. Name one discriminator before naming the structure.","checks":[["Which coat is vascular and pigmented immediately external to the retina?","Choroid."],["Which retinal cells are the photoreceptors?","Rods and cones."]],"seed":{"title":"Choroid, retina and accessory eye structures","minimumModel":"The eye has fibrous, vascular and neural layers. Choroid is vascular/pigmented; retina is laminated nervous tissue with photoreceptors, bipolar/interneuronal layers and ganglion-cell axons. Accessory structures include conjunctiva, eyelids and lacrimal apparatus.","confusions":["choroid vs retina vs sclera","pigment epithelium vs photoreceptor layer","lacrimal gland vs conjunctiva"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Identify choroid and retinal layers from outside-to-inside organization and name the major accessory structures.","transferPrompt":"Given a histologic section, distinguish retina, choroid and sclera by tissue architecture.","visualAnchor":"Layered globe section plus accessory eye overview."}},"T054":{"id":"T054","topic":"Cerebellum physiology","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Cerebellum physiology as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Cerebellum compares intended motor commands with sensory feedback and adjusts timing, force and sequence.","Vestibulocerebellum controls equilibrium/eye movement; spinocerebellum ongoing axial/limb control; cerebrocerebellum planning and skilled movement.","Cerebellar lesions produce ipsilateral dysmetria, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia and gait/stance abnormalities rather than primary weakness."],"mental":"Cerebellar signs are errors of coordination, timing and calibration.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Make division→input/output→clinical deficit table.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Classic cerebellar tremor type?","Intention tremor."],["Cerebellar lesion weakness?","Usually no primary paralysis; coordination is impaired."]],"seed":null},"T055":{"id":"T055","topic":"Orbit I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Orbit I-II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Orbit contains globe, extraocular muscles, optic nerve, ophthalmic artery, cranial nerves controlling eye movement and V1 sensory branches.","III supplies most extraocular muscles plus levator; IV supplies superior oblique; VI supplies lateral rectus.","Common tendinous ring relationship helps predict which structures enter within vs outside the ring at superior orbital fissure."],"mental":"Eye-movement mnemonic: LR6 SO4, all the rest 3.","imaginePrompt":"Picture light entering the eye, being focused onto retina, converted into neural activity, then crossing at the chiasm toward cortex.","draw":"Draw orbit apex with optic canal and superior orbital fissure contents.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Lateral rectus innervation?","CN VI."],["Superior oblique innervation?","CN IV."]],"seed":{"title":"Orbit I","minimumModel":"The orbit is a pyramidal bony cavity with base anterior and apex posterior. Its walls and fissures/canals constrain the globe, extraocular muscles, optic nerve, ophthalmic vessels and cranial nerves.","confusions":["optic canal vs superior orbital fissure","roof vs medial wall","orbit vs pterygopalatine fossa"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Draw orbital walls and apex, then place optic canal, superior orbital fissure and inferior orbital fissure.","transferPrompt":"Predict which structures are threatened by a lesion near the orbital apex.","visualAnchor":"Bony orbit with walls and openings."}},"T056":{"id":"T056","topic":"Orbit II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Master the orbital contents as a functional arrangement: extraocular muscles, ocular motor nerves, optic pathway, vessels and lesion patterns.","teach":["The four recti arise from the common tendinous ring and form a muscular cone around the optic nerve. Superior and inferior obliques follow different courses and actions.","Motor innervation follows the compact rule LR6 SO4, all others III: lateral rectus by abducens, superior oblique by trochlear, and the remaining major extraocular muscles by oculomotor nerve.","The optic nerve and ophthalmic artery pass through the optic canal. CN III, IV, V1 and VI use the superior orbital fissure, with important differences in relation to the common tendinous ring.","Eye movement is solved by vectors rather than memorized lists: identify the starting position, the muscle's line of pull and which action isolates that muscle clinically.","Lesions are pattern questions: a nerve palsy produces a characteristic resting eye position, movement deficit and sometimes pupil/eyelid findings."],"mental":"Orbit II = muscular cone + optic canal + superior orbital fissure + LR6 SO4 all others III.","imaginePrompt":"Look into the orbit from the front. Build the rectus cone around the optic nerve, then add obliques and route CN III/IV/VI to their target muscles.","draw":"Sketch the orbit with four recti, two obliques, optic nerve and ocular motor nerves.","examGrammar":"Expect muscle action, nerve lesion, fissure/canal contents and relation-to-ring questions.","practicalCue":"Orient the orbital apex and optic nerve first; then identify muscle by position and line of pull.","checks":[["Which nerve supplies lateral rectus?","Abducens nerve (CN VI)."],["Which nerve supplies superior oblique?","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)."]],"seed":{"title":"Orbit II","minimumModel":"Extraocular muscles are organized around the globe and common tendinous ring; CN III supplies most, CN IV supplies superior oblique, and CN VI supplies lateral rectus. Orbital nerves and vessels follow predictable relations to the muscles and optic nerve.","confusions":["superior oblique vs inferior oblique","lateral rectus vs medial rectus","CN III vs IV vs VI deficits"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct muscle actions and III/IV/VI innervation, then place major nerves at the orbital apex.","transferPrompt":"Localize an isolated failure of abduction, depression in adduction, or multiple ocular movements.","visualAnchor":"Orbit with extraocular muscles and motor cranial nerves."}},"T057":{"id":"T057","topic":"Fascia of neck, SCM, posterior triangle, EJV and cervical plexus","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Fascia neck SCM posterior triangle EJV cervical plexus and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Investing deep cervical fascia encloses SCM/trapezius and helps define neck spaces; visceral and prevertebral layers matter for spread of infection.","Posterior triangle boundaries: SCM anteriorly, trapezius posteriorly, clavicle inferiorly. CN XI is vulnerable as it crosses the triangle.","External jugular vein crosses superficially over SCM; cervical plexus cutaneous branches emerge near midpoint of posterior border of SCM."],"mental":"Posterior triangle is a surface map for XI, EJV and cervical plexus.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw triangle boundaries and Erb point/cutaneous branches.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Nerve vulnerable in posterior triangle?","Accessory nerve (XI)."],["EJV course relative to SCM?","Superficial to SCM."]],"seed":null},"T058":{"id":"T058","topic":"Thalamus, hypothalamus and arousal","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Thalamus hypothalamus arousal as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Thalamus relays and filters sensory/motor information to cortex and participates in attention/arousal networks.","Hypothalamus coordinates autonomic/endocrine/homeostatic behavior including temperature, hunger, thirst and circadian rhythms.","Ascending reticular activating systems and thalamocortical activity contribute to wakefulness and consciousness."],"mental":"Thalamus routes; hypothalamus regulates; reticular system activates.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw thalamus-hypothalamus-reticular-cortex arousal triangle.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Major role of thalamus?","Relay/integration for cortical information."],["Suprachiasmatic nucleus role?","Circadian timing."]],"seed":{"title":"Thalamus, hypothalamus and arousal","minimumModel":"The thalamus is a major relay/modulation hub for sensory, motor and associative information, whereas hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine and homeostatic drives. Ascending reticular/arousal systems interact with thalamus, hypothalamus and cortex to regulate wakefulness and attention.","confusions":["thalamus vs hypothalamus","specific relay nuclei vs reticular/intralaminar nuclei","arousal vs ordinary sensory transmission"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Contrast thalamic relay functions with hypothalamic homeostasis, then trace the concept of ascending arousal to cortex.","transferPrompt":"Predict broad consequences of bilateral arousal-system failure versus a focal sensory thalamic lesion.","visualAnchor":"Diencephalon map with thalamus, hypothalamus and ascending arousal arrows."}},"T059":{"id":"T059","topic":"EEG, sleep and memory","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain EEG sleep and memory as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["EEG records summed cortical electrical activity. Alpha rhythms are prominent in relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed; deeper NREM sleep progressively shows slower activity, while REM has low-amplitude mixed-frequency activity with muscle atonia.","NREM and REM cycle repeatedly; REM is associated with vivid dreaming and autonomic variability.","Sleep supports memory consolidation; deprivation impairs attention, working memory and learning efficiency."],"mental":"Sleep is part of the study protocol because it changes encoding and consolidation, not just comfort.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Sketch wake → N1 → N2 → N3 → REM cycle.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Deep slow-wave sleep corresponds mainly to?","N3 NREM."],["REM muscle tone?","Marked skeletal muscle atonia except essential/ocular muscles."]],"seed":{"title":"EEG, sleep and memory","minimumModel":"EEG reflects synchronized cortical electrical activity. Wakefulness, NREM stages and REM have characteristic patterns; normal sleep cycles repeatedly through NREM and REM, while sleep contributes to memory processing and consolidation.","confusions":["alpha vs beta activity","NREM stages vs REM","sleep architecture vs subjective sleep quality"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct major sleep stages and their broad EEG signatures, then connect sleep to memory consolidation.","transferPrompt":"Given sleep deprivation before learning or retrieval, predict why performance may degrade without inventing a fixed REM quota.","visualAnchor":"Simplified hypnogram plus representative EEG patterns."}},"T060":{"id":"T060","topic":"Molecular mechanism of memory and speech","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Molecular memory and speech as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Long-term potentiation is a durable increase in synaptic efficacy and is a major cellular model for memory; NMDA-dependent Ca2+ signaling is important in many hippocampal circuits.","Declarative memory depends strongly on medial temporal/hippocampal systems, while skills recruit distributed cortical/basal ganglia/cerebellar circuits.","Dominant hemisphere language network classically includes Broca-related frontal production systems and Wernicke-related posterior temporal comprehension systems connected by white-matter pathways."],"mental":"Memory is network-specific; language deficits localize by production, comprehension and repetition.","imaginePrompt":"Turn the mechanism into moving arrows. Each arrow must have a cause, a direction and a consequence.","draw":"Draw Broca–arcuate–Wernicke triangle and one LTP sketch.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Broca lesion typically causes?","Nonfluent expressive language impairment with relatively preserved comprehension."],["Hippocampus is critical for?","Formation/consolidation of declarative memories."]],"seed":null},"T061":{"id":"T061","topic":"Subclavian vessels, scalenes and suboccipital region","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Subclavian vessels scalenes suboccipital region and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Subclavian artery is divided into three parts by anterior scalene; brachial plexus and subclavian artery pass between anterior and middle scalenes, while subclavian vein passes anterior to anterior scalene.","Vertebral artery arises from subclavian and ascends through transverse foramina to enter skull via foramen magnum.","Suboccipital triangle contains vertebral artery and suboccipital nerve deep to posterior neck muscles."],"mental":"Anterior scalene separates vein from artery/plexus.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw scalene triangle and suboccipital triangle contents.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Subclavian vein relation to anterior scalene?","Anterior to it."],["Subclavian artery and brachial plexus pass where?","Between anterior and middle scalene muscles."]],"seed":null},"T062":{"id":"T062","topic":"Anterior, carotid and muscular triangles of the neck","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Anterior carotid muscular triangles and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Anterior triangle is subdivided into submental, submandibular, carotid and muscular triangles.","Carotid triangle contains common carotid bifurcation, internal/external carotid segments, IJV and vagus within carotid sheath region plus nearby hypoglossal nerve.","Muscular triangle overlies infrahyoid muscles, thyroid, larynx/trachea."],"mental":"Neck triangles are location maps, not lists: boundaries predict contents.","imaginePrompt":"Trace blood from origin → course → named branches → territory. Then remove one branch and predict the deficit/ischemic territory.","draw":"Draw anterior triangle subdivisions and carotid triangle contents.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Common carotid bifurcation is found in which triangle?","Carotid triangle."],["Major muscle group in muscular triangle?","Infrahyoid muscles."]],"seed":null},"T063":{"id":"T063","topic":"Thyroid gland","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Thyroid gland and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Thyroid lobes flank larynx/trachea and are joined by isthmus, commonly over upper tracheal rings.","Superior thyroid artery arises from ECA; inferior thyroid artery from thyrocervical trunk. Recurrent laryngeal nerves closely relate to inferior thyroid region; external laryngeal nerve relates to superior thyroid vessels.","Parathyroids usually lie posteriorly and must be preserved in thyroid surgery."],"mental":"Thyroid surgery is a nerve-and-blood-supply anatomy problem.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw superior/inferior thyroid vessels and recurrent/external laryngeal nerves.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Nerve endangered near inferior thyroid artery?","Recurrent laryngeal nerve."],["External laryngeal nerve is at risk with?","Superior thyroid vessels."]],"seed":null},"T064":{"id":"T064","topic":"Optics, eye fluid and intraocular pressure","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Optics eye fluid and IOP as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Cornea provides most refractive power; lens fine-tunes focus by accommodation. Near vision requires increased lens curvature via ciliary muscle action.","Aqueous humor is produced by ciliary processes, flows posterior chamber → pupil → anterior chamber and drains mainly through trabecular meshwork/Schlemm canal.","Raised IOP can damage optic nerve in glaucoma."],"mental":"Cornea bends most; lens adjusts; aqueous drainage controls pressure.","imaginePrompt":"Picture light entering the eye, being focused onto retina, converted into neural activity, then crossing at the chiasm toward cortex.","draw":"Draw aqueous humor flow and accommodation mechanism.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Main conventional aqueous outflow route?","Trabecular meshwork to canal of Schlemm."],["What changes lens shape for near vision?","Ciliary muscle via zonular tension changes."]],"seed":{"title":"Optics, eye fluid and intraocular pressure","minimumModel":"Light is refracted mainly by the cornea and further focused by the lens. Aqueous humor is produced by ciliary processes, flows through posterior chamber → pupil → anterior chamber and exits mainly through trabecular meshwork/Schlemm pathway; resistance to outflow influences intraocular pressure.","confusions":["aqueous vs vitreous humor","production vs outflow","corneal power vs lens accommodation"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace light focusing and aqueous-humor circulation as two causal chains.","transferPrompt":"Predict how impaired aqueous outflow affects IOP and why this is different from a refractive error.","visualAnchor":"Eye optical path plus aqueous-flow arrows."}},"T065":{"id":"T065","topic":"Submandibular region I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Submandibular region I-II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Submandibular triangle contains submandibular gland, facial vessels and nodes; gland wraps around posterior border of mylohyoid with superficial and deep parts.","Submandibular duct runs forward in floor of mouth and opens beside lingual frenulum. Lingual nerve loops in close relation to the duct.","Parasympathetic secretomotor fibers originate from facial nerve chorda tympani, join lingual nerve and synapse in submandibular ganglion."],"mental":"Chorda tympani rides lingual nerve to submandibular ganglion.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw duct–lingual nerve relation and parasympathetic route.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Preganglionic secretomotor source for submandibular gland?","Facial nerve via chorda tympani."],["Submandibular duct opens where?","Sublingual papilla beside lingual frenulum."]],"seed":{"title":"Submandibular region I","minimumModel":"The submandibular triangle contains the submandibular gland and important facial/lingual neurovascular relations. The gland wraps around the posterior border of mylohyoid, and its duct runs forward in the floor of mouth.","confusions":["submandibular vs sublingual gland","superficial vs deep gland part","Wharton duct vs parotid duct"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map triangle boundaries, gland superficial/deep parts and duct course relative to mylohyoid.","transferPrompt":"Predict where a submandibular duct stone may cause pain/swelling and which nearby structures matter surgically.","visualAnchor":"Floor-of-mouth view with mylohyoid, gland and duct."}},"T066":{"id":"T066","topic":"Submandibular region II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Turn the submandibular region into one relation map linking gland, duct, lingual nerve, hypoglossal nerve and secretomotor pathway.","teach":["The submandibular gland has superficial and deep parts that wrap around the posterior border of mylohyoid. Its duct arises from the deep part and runs forward in the floor of the mouth.","The lingual nerve has a memorable three-dimensional relationship to the submandibular duct: it passes lateral, then inferior, then medial to the duct as it loops beneath it.","The hypoglossal nerve runs deeper in the floor of mouth on the hyoglossus surface, while the lingual nerve is more superior and closely tied to the duct and submandibular ganglion.","Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers for the gland travel with chorda tympani, join the lingual nerve and synapse in the submandibular ganglion; postganglionic fibers supply submandibular and sublingual glands.","Exam questions love relation reversals here: duct↔lingual nerve, gland↔mylohyoid, ganglion↔lingual nerve and lesion→salivary consequence."],"mental":"Deep gland gives duct; lingual nerve loops under it; chorda tympani rides lingual nerve to the submandibular ganglion.","imaginePrompt":"View the floor of mouth from the side. Slide the duct forward, then loop the lingual nerve beneath it and place the ganglion hanging from the nerve.","draw":"Draw mylohyoid, superficial/deep gland, duct, lingual nerve loop, submandibular ganglion and hypoglossal nerve.","examGrammar":"Expect duct/nerve relations, parasympathetic route, gland parts and floor-of-mouth applied anatomy.","practicalCue":"Identify mylohyoid and gland first, then trace the duct and locate lingual/hypoglossal nerves by their relations.","checks":[["How does the lingual nerve relate to the submandibular duct?","It loops from lateral to inferior to medial around the duct."],["Where do parasympathetic secretomotor fibers synapse before supplying the submandibular gland?","Submandibular ganglion."]],"seed":{"title":"Submandibular region II","minimumModel":"Parasympathetic secretomotor fibers to submandibular/sublingual glands travel with chorda tympani, join lingual nerve and synapse in the submandibular ganglion. Lingual nerve and hypoglossal nerve have distinct relations and functions in the floor of mouth.","confusions":["lingual vs hypoglossal nerve","submandibular ganglion vs gland","taste fibers vs general sensation"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace chorda tympani → lingual nerve → submandibular ganglion → glands, then place lingual and hypoglossal nerves around the duct.","transferPrompt":"Separate taste/secretomotor and tongue-motor deficits after different floor-of-mouth nerve injuries.","visualAnchor":"Floor-of-mouth nerve/duct relation diagram."}},"T067":{"id":"T067","topic":"Cornea, lens and refraction","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Understand cornea and lens as transparent optical tissues whose histology explains refraction and accommodation.","teach":["The cornea is avascular and transparent. Its major layers are surface epithelium, Bowman's layer, highly regular collagenous stroma, Descemet's membrane and corneal endothelium.","Transparency depends on orderly stromal collagen spacing, controlled hydration and absence of blood vessels. Disruption of stromal organization or endothelial pump function can produce edema and loss of clarity.","The cornea provides most of the eye's fixed refractive power because light crosses a large refractive-index change at the air-cornea interface.","The lens is a transparent biconvex structure enclosed by a thick capsule. Anterior lens epithelium gives rise to elongated lens fibers that lose nuclei and organelles as they mature, reducing light scatter.","Accommodation changes lens curvature through ciliary muscle action and zonular tension. The lens changes shape; the cornea does not perform accommodation."],"mental":"Cornea = fixed clear refracting window; lens = adjustable transparent refractor.","imaginePrompt":"Follow a ray of light through the corneal layers, aqueous humor and lens, then make the lens rounder for near vision.","draw":"Draw the five corneal layers and a lens with capsule, anterior epithelium and lens fibers.","examGrammar":"Expect corneal-layer order, avascularity/transparency mechanisms, lens-fiber maturation and accommodation relationships.","practicalCue":"Cornea is recognized by its layered anterior wall and thick stroma; lens by a capsule surrounding tightly packed fibers.","checks":[["Which corneal layer provides most thickness?","Stroma."],["What changes during accommodation?","Lens curvature/refractive power."]],"seed":null},"T068":{"id":"T068","topic":"Iris and retina","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Recognize iris and retina, then use the retinal layers to understand photoreception and visual signal flow.","teach":["The iris is a vascular pigmented diaphragm that controls pupil size. Its stroma contains vessels and melanocytes, while the posterior surface is heavily pigmented.","The sphincter pupillae constricts the pupil under parasympathetic control; the dilator pupillae enlarges it under sympathetic control.","The retina is organized so light passes through inner neural layers before reaching rods and cones adjacent to the retinal pigment epithelium. The photoreceptors then signal bipolar cells, which signal ganglion cells.","Ganglion-cell axons run along the inner retina and converge at the optic disc to form the optic nerve. The optic disc has no photoreceptors, creating the physiologic blind spot.","The macula/fovea is specialized for high visual acuity, with dense cones and displacement of overlying layers to reduce optical interference."],"mental":"Light travels inward-to-outward to photoreceptors; neural information then travels back outward through bipolar → ganglion → optic nerve.","imaginePrompt":"Send a photon through the retina to a cone, then reverse direction for the neural signal leaving through the optic nerve.","draw":"Draw simplified retinal layers with pigment epithelium, rods/cones, bipolar cells and ganglion cells, plus the iris sphincter/dilator.","examGrammar":"Common traps: direction of light vs neural transmission, rods vs cones, optic disc vs fovea and parasympathetic vs sympathetic pupil control.","practicalCue":"Identify retinal pigment epithelium/choroid side first, then the layered neural retina and vitreous-facing ganglion side.","checks":[["Which cells are retinal photoreceptors?","Rods and cones."],["Why is the optic disc a blind spot?","It lacks photoreceptors where ganglion axons exit."]],"seed":null},"T069":{"id":"T069","topic":"Common and internal carotid arteries","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Common and internal carotid arteries and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Common carotid bifurcates into internal and external carotids. Carotid sinus is a baroreceptor region; carotid body senses blood gases/pH.","Internal carotid has no branches in the neck and enters skull to contribute to anterior cerebral circulation, including ACA and MCA.","Clinical carotid disease can cause retinal or hemispheric ischemic symptoms."],"mental":"ICA is the neck 'silent pipe' to brain; ECA branches in neck/face.","imaginePrompt":"Trace blood from origin → course → named branches → territory. Then remove one branch and predict the deficit/ischemic territory.","draw":"Draw carotid bifurcation with sinus/body and ICA intracranial continuation.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Branches of ICA in neck?","Normally none."],["Carotid sinus function?","Baroreception."]],"seed":{"title":"Common and internal carotid arteries","minimumModel":"The common carotid ascends in the carotid sheath and bifurcates into external and internal carotid arteries. The cervical internal carotid has no branches, ascends to the skull base, and is closely related to IJV and vagus; carotid sinus/body lie near the bifurcation.","confusions":["ICA vs ECA branches in neck","carotid sinus vs body","artery vs IJV vs vagus relation"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace CCA to ICA and map sheath relations, bifurcation, sinus and body.","transferPrompt":"Predict why an ICA lesion in the neck differs from an ECA lesion in facial blood supply.","visualAnchor":"Carotid sheath and bifurcation diagram."}},"T070":{"id":"T070","topic":"Theory of vision and phototransduction","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Theory of vision and phototransduction as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["In darkness, cGMP-gated channels remain open and photoreceptors are relatively depolarized. Light activates opsin → transducin → phosphodiesterase, lowers cGMP, closes channels and hyperpolarizes the photoreceptor.","Rods are highly light-sensitive but low acuity/color; cones support daylight, color and high acuity.","Retinal circuits use bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells before ganglion output."],"mental":"Light makes photoreceptors hyperpolarize, which feels backwards but is exam gold.","imaginePrompt":"Picture light entering the eye, being focused onto retina, converted into neural activity, then crossing at the chiasm toward cortex.","draw":"Draw dark vs light phototransduction cascade.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Effect of light on photoreceptor membrane potential?","Hyperpolarization."],["Second messenger reduced by light?","cGMP."]],"seed":{"title":"Theory of vision and phototransduction","minimumModel":"In darkness photoreceptor cGMP-gated channels support a depolarized state and continuous transmitter release. Light activates opsin/transducin/PDE signaling, lowers cGMP, closes channels and hyperpolarizes the photoreceptor, changing downstream retinal signaling.","confusions":["dark current vs light response","photoreceptor hyperpolarization vs ordinary sensory depolarization","rod vs cone roles"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct dark state and light-triggered phototransduction in causal order.","transferPrompt":"Predict the direction of photoreceptor membrane-potential change when light increases.","visualAnchor":"Rod/cone membrane signaling cascade."}},"T071":{"id":"T071","topic":"Color vision, visual pathway and visual field","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Color vision visual pathway visual field as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Cone opsins with different spectral sensitivities support color vision; opponent processing refines color contrast.","Nasal retinal fibers cross at optic chiasm; temporal retinal fibers remain ipsilateral. Each optic tract therefore carries the contralateral visual hemifield.","Lesion patterns: optic nerve monocular loss; chiasm bitemporal hemianopia; tract/radiation/visual cortex contralateral homonymous defects."],"mental":"Field defects are solved by mapping visual field → retina → chiasm crossing.","imaginePrompt":"Mentally travel with the signal: receptor → neuron order → synapse → crossing → destination. Rotate the pathway and ask what a lesion before/after the crossing would do.","draw":"Draw pathway and mark lesions at nerve, chiasm, tract, radiations, cortex.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Which retinal fibers cross?","Nasal retinal fibers."],["Chiasmal lesion classically causes?","Bitemporal hemianopia."]],"seed":null},"T072":{"id":"T072","topic":"External carotid artery and internal jugular vein","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of External carotid artery and internal jugular vein and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["ECA supplies much of face/scalp and gives superior thyroid, ascending pharyngeal, lingual, facial, occipital, posterior auricular, maxillary and superficial temporal branches.","IJV begins at jugular foramen as continuation of sigmoid sinus and descends in carotid sheath, usually lateral to carotid artery with vagus between/posterior.","The carotid sheath organizes artery, vein and vagus into a clinically important neck bundle."],"mental":"ECA branches outward; IJV drains inward cranial/neck venous blood.","imaginePrompt":"Trace blood from origin → course → named branches → territory. Then remove one branch and predict the deficit/ischemic territory.","draw":"Draw ECA branch tree and carotid sheath cross-section.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Terminal ECA branches?","Maxillary and superficial temporal arteries."],["Largest major deep vein in carotid sheath?","Internal jugular vein."]],"seed":{"title":"External carotid artery and internal jugular vein","minimumModel":"The external carotid supplies much of the extracranial head/neck through named branches, while the internal jugular vein begins at the jugular foramen, descends in the carotid sheath lateral to the carotid artery and joins the subclavian vein.","confusions":["ECA vs ICA","IJV vs EJV","superior thyroid vs inferior thyroid vessels"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct ECA branches in a spatial order and trace IJV from skull base to brachiocephalic vein.","transferPrompt":"Predict which structures are encountered in a carotid-sheath procedure and how artery/vein positions differ.","visualAnchor":"Neck arterial tree beside IJV course."}},"T073":{"id":"T073","topic":"Facial nerve and submandibular ganglion","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Facial nerve and submandibular ganglion and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Facial nerve carries branchial motor to facial expression, parasympathetic fibers to lacrimal/submandibular/sublingual glands, taste from anterior two-thirds tongue and small somatic sensory component.","Chorda tympani leaves facial nerve, crosses middle ear, joins lingual nerve and carries taste plus preganglionic parasympathetics to submandibular ganglion.","Lesion level determines whether lacrimation, stapedius, taste and salivation are affected in addition to facial movement."],"mental":"Facial nerve is a mixed cable; lesion localization depends on which branches have already departed.","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Draw intratemporal branches: greater petrosal, nerve to stapedius, chorda tympani, then extracranial motor branches.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Chorda tympani carries what?","Taste anterior 2/3 tongue and preganglionic parasympathetics for submandibular/sublingual glands."],["Facial expression motor branchial nerve?","CN VII."]],"seed":{"title":"Facial nerve and submandibular ganglion","minimumModel":"CN VII carries branchial motor fibers to facial-expression muscles and parasympathetic/taste fibers through distinct branches. Chorda tympani joins lingual nerve to carry taste from anterior tongue and preganglionic secretomotor fibers to the submandibular ganglion.","confusions":["facial motor branches vs chorda tympani","CN VII vs lingual nerve","submandibular vs otic ganglion"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Separate facial motor branches from chorda-tympani functions and trace the submandibular parasympathetic route.","transferPrompt":"Localize a lesion from facial weakness plus taste/salivation findings.","visualAnchor":"Facial nerve branch map plus chorda/lingual/ganglion route."}},"T074":{"id":"T074","topic":"Glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["CN IX supplies taste/sensation posterior one-third tongue, stylopharyngeus motor, carotid sinus/body afferents and parotid secretomotor pathway.","CN X supplies palate/pharynx/larynx branchial motor (except classic exceptions), parasympathetics to thoracoabdominal viscera and visceral/somatic sensory pathways.","Both exit jugular foramen and participate in swallowing/gag-related circuits."],"mental":"IX is compact but diverse; X is the long visceral/branchial workhorse.","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Create IX vs X function table and trace jugular foramen exit.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Motor muscle uniquely associated with CN IX?","Stylopharyngeus."],["Major parasympathetic cranial nerve to thorax/abdomen?","Vagus nerve."]],"seed":null},"T075":{"id":"T075","topic":"Accessory, hypoglossal and cervical sympathetic trunk","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Accessory hypoglossal and cervical sympathetic trunk and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["CN XI supplies SCM and trapezius; injury causes weak head turn and shoulder shrug.","CN XII supplies intrinsic/extrinsic tongue muscles except palatoglossus; LMN lesion causes tongue deviation toward weak side.","Cervical sympathetic trunk contains superior, middle and inferior cervical ganglia; postganglionics reach head largely via periarterial plexuses. Disruption can cause Horner syndrome."],"mental":"XI = shoulder/head; XII = tongue; sympathetic trunk = pupil/eyelid/sweat/vasomotor.","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Draw XI/XII courses and sympathetic chain beside carotid sheath.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Tongue deviation in XII LMN lesion?","Toward lesion side."],["Horner syndrome triad concept?","Miosis, ptosis and reduced facial sweating from sympathetic interruption."]],"seed":null},"T076":{"id":"T076","topic":"Ear and vestibulocochlear nerve","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Ear and vestibulocochlear nerve and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["CN VIII has cochlear and vestibular divisions. Peripheral hearing signals originate in spiral ganglion; balance signals in vestibular ganglion.","Auditory information projects bilaterally through brainstem nuclei, which is why central unilateral lesions above cochlear nuclei rarely cause complete unilateral deafness.","Vestibular outputs coordinate gaze, posture and perception of head motion."],"mental":"VIII is two sensors sharing one nerve: sound and head motion.","imaginePrompt":"Trace the nerve from origin/nuclei through skull exit, course, branches and target territory as one continuous wire.","draw":"Draw cochlear vs vestibular branches and central targets.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Ganglion for cochlear afferents?","Spiral ganglion."],["Why central auditory lesions often spare one ear partially?","Bilateral central projections."]],"seed":{"title":"Ear and vestibulocochlear nerve","minimumModel":"CN VIII has cochlear and vestibular divisions. Cochlear fibers relay auditory signals from spiral ganglion/cochlea to cochlear nuclei and onward bilaterally; vestibular fibers carry signals from canals/otolith organs to vestibular nuclei and cerebellum.","confusions":["cochlear vs vestibular division","spiral vs vestibular ganglion","peripheral vs central auditory lesion"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace cochlear and vestibular divisions from receptors to first central relays.","transferPrompt":"Predict why a peripheral CN VIII lesion can impair hearing and balance together.","visualAnchor":"Inner ear connected to CN VIII divisions and brainstem nuclei."}},"T077":{"id":"T077","topic":"External and middle ear with labyrinth","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a usable exam model of External and middle ear with labyrinth, not a paragraph memory.","teach":["The external ear funnels sound from auricle through the external acoustic meatus to the tympanic membrane. The membrane is the mechanical boundary between external and middle ear, and its vibration is the first step in converting airborne sound into ossicular motion.","The ossicles form a chain from malleus to incus to stapes. The malleus is attached to the tympanic membrane; the stapes footplate sits in the oval window. Their lever action and the large tympanic-membrane area relative to the stapes footplate increase pressure delivered to inner-ear fluid.","The auditory tube connects the middle ear to the nasopharynx. Opening of the tube equalizes pressure across the tympanic membrane, which is why tube dysfunction can cause discomfort and conductive hearing problems.","The middle ear communicates posteriorly with mastoid air cells through the aditus. Clinically, this connection explains how middle-ear infection can spread into the mastoid system.","The bony labyrinth contains cochlea, vestibule and semicircular canals and is filled with perilymph. Inside it lies the membranous labyrinth, which contains endolymph. Cochlear components serve hearing; vestibular sacs and semicircular ducts serve linear and angular acceleration.","Conductive hearing loss means sound is not being mechanically delivered efficiently through the external/middle ear. Sensorineural loss means the cochlea, hair cells, CN VIII or central auditory pathway is impaired."],"mental":"Outer ear collects → middle ear transmits/amplifies → inner ear transduces and senses balance.","imaginePrompt":"Follow one sound wave without skipping a structure: auricle → canal → tympanic membrane → malleus → incus → stapes → oval window → cochlear fluid. Then switch to head rotation and follow the vestibular apparatus instead.","draw":"Draw pinna → tympanic membrane → ossicles → oval window → cochlea.","examGrammar":"Trace sound transmission or distinguish conductive vs sensorineural problems.","practicalCue":"On a temporal-bone or ear model, orient external canal, tympanic membrane, ossicles, auditory tube, mastoid cavity and oval/round windows before naming inner-ear compartments.","checks":[["Which ossicle directly contacts the oval window?","Stapes."],["Which labyrinth component detects angular acceleration?","Semicircular ducts/canals via the cristae ampullares."]],"seed":{"title":"External ear, middle ear and labyrinth","minimumModel":"External ear funnels sound to tympanic membrane; middle-ear ossicles transmit/amplify vibration toward the oval window and auditory tube equalizes pressure. Inner-ear bony labyrinth contains cochlea, vestibule and semicircular canals around the membranous labyrinth.","confusions":["external vs middle vs inner ear","oval vs round window","bony vs membranous labyrinth"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace sound mechanically from auricle to oval window, then name major bony-labyrinth compartments.","transferPrompt":"Predict the difference between a conductive problem in middle ear and a sensorineural problem in cochlea/CN VIII.","visualAnchor":"Section from auricle through middle ear to cochlea/labyrinth."}},"T078":{"id":"T078","topic":"Organ of Corti and sound transmission","subject":"HISTOLOGY","objective":"Understand the organ of Corti as a mechanical-to-electrical transducer and connect its histology to sound transmission.","teach":["The cochlear duct lies between scala vestibuli and scala tympani and contains endolymph. Its floor includes the basilar membrane, which supports the organ of Corti.","The organ of Corti contains one row of inner hair cells, three rows of outer hair cells and specialized supporting cells. Hair-cell stereocilia interact mechanically with movement between the basilar and tectorial membranes.","Sound transmitted through tympanic membrane and ossicles drives the stapes at the oval window, producing fluid waves in the cochlea and frequency-specific displacement of the basilar membrane.","Deflection of hair-cell stereocilia opens mechanically gated channels. Because endolymph is potassium-rich and has a positive endocochlear potential, potassium entry depolarizes hair cells and promotes transmitter release onto cochlear nerve fibers.","Inner hair cells provide most afferent auditory signaling. Outer hair cells actively change length and amplify/sharpen basilar-membrane motion, improving sensitivity and frequency selectivity."],"mental":"Sound → ossicles → cochlear fluid wave → basilar membrane motion → stereocilia deflection → hair-cell depolarization → CN VIII.","imaginePrompt":"Push the stapes into the oval window and follow one wave until a hair bundle bends and an auditory nerve fiber fires.","draw":"Draw scala vestibuli, cochlear duct, scala tympani, basilar membrane, tectorial membrane and inner/outer hair cells.","examGrammar":"Expect endolymph/perilymph swaps, inner-vs-outer hair-cell function, membrane relationships and the sequence of mechanical transduction.","practicalCue":"Recognize the triangular cochlear duct and locate the organ of Corti on the basilar membrane beneath the tectorial membrane.","checks":[["Where is the organ of Corti located?","On the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct."],["Which hair cells provide most auditory afferent output?","Inner hair cells."]],"seed":null},"T079":{"id":"T079","topic":"Oral cavity and tongue","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Oral cavity and tongue and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Tongue motor is XII except palatoglossus (X). General sensation anterior 2/3 is lingual V3, taste there via chorda tympani VII; posterior 1/3 both general sensation and taste mainly IX.","Lingual artery supplies tongue; deep lingual veins are visible ventrally.","Floor of mouth contains sublingual gland and submandibular duct relations."],"mental":"Tongue exams are a nerve-routing puzzle: V3 touch, VII taste, IX posterior, XII motor.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw tongue sensory/taste/motor innervation map.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Taste anterior 2/3?","Chorda tympani via facial nerve."],["General sensation anterior 2/3?","Lingual nerve, V3."]],"seed":{"title":"Oral cavity and tongue","minimumModel":"The oral cavity is divided into vestibule and oral cavity proper. Tongue muscles are intrinsic/extrinsic, motor supply is mainly CN XII except palatoglossus via X, while general sensation and taste vary by anterior versus posterior tongue regions.","confusions":["lingual nerve vs chorda tympani","CN IX vs X posterior/root territory","intrinsic vs extrinsic tongue muscles"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map tongue motor supply and separate general sensation from taste by tongue region.","transferPrompt":"Localize a nerve lesion from tongue deviation, anterior taste loss, or posterior sensory loss.","visualAnchor":"Tongue innervation map and floor of mouth."}},"T080":{"id":"T080","topic":"Soft palate and pharynx I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Soft palate and pharynx I-II and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Soft palate separates naso- and oropharynx during swallowing; most palatal muscles are vagal via pharyngeal plexus, while tensor veli palatini is V3.","Pharyngeal constrictors propel bolus; stylopharyngeus is CN IX, most other pharyngeal motor is CN X.","Waldeyer ring of lymphoid tissue surrounds pharyngeal entrance."],"mental":"Exception pair: tensor palate = V3; stylopharyngeus = IX; most remaining palate/pharynx motor = X.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw pharyngeal wall and motor exceptions.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Tensor veli palatini innervation?","V3."],["Stylopharyngeus innervation?","CN IX."]],"seed":{"title":"Soft palate and pharynx I","minimumModel":"The soft palate closes the nasopharynx during swallowing and is moved mainly by muscles supplied through the pharyngeal plexus, except tensor veli palatini via V3. The pharynx is divided into naso-, oro- and laryngopharynx.","confusions":["tensor vs levator veli palatini","nasopharynx vs oropharynx vs laryngopharynx","V3 exception vs vagal supply"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map soft-palate muscles/innervation and the three pharyngeal regions.","transferPrompt":"Predict palate movement and nasal regurgitation risk after vagal/pharyngeal-plexus weakness.","visualAnchor":"Soft palate and sagittal pharynx."}},"T081":{"id":"T081","topic":"Pharynx II","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a usable exam model of Pharynx II, not a paragraph memory.","teach":["The pharynx is a muscular tube extending from the skull base to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, where it continues as the esophagus. It is divided into nasopharynx, oropharynx and laryngopharynx.","The circular layer is formed by superior, middle and inferior constrictors. During swallowing they contract sequentially to propel the bolus downward while helping close gaps in the pharyngeal wall.","The longitudinal muscles, stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus, elevate and shorten the pharynx during swallowing.","Motor supply is mainly through the vagus nerve via the pharyngeal plexus. Stylopharyngeus is the memorable exception: it is supplied by glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX.","Sensory supply changes by level: nasopharynx mainly V2, oropharynx mainly IX, and laryngopharynx mainly internal laryngeal branches of X. That regional map explains why sensory deficits localize differently from motor weakness."],"mental":"Pharynx is a muscular tube organized by three levels and a plexus.","imaginePrompt":"Picture a swallowed bolus entering the oropharynx while the pharynx elevates, then watch the constrictors squeeze from superior to inferior.","draw":"Draw naso/oro/laryngopharynx with soft palate and major constrictors.","examGrammar":"Boundaries, muscles, nerve supply and swallowing deficit.","practicalCue":"On a posterior pharyngeal view, identify the three constrictors by overlapping arrangement and then locate the pharyngeal plexus on the outer wall.","checks":[["Which pharyngeal muscle is supplied by CN IX?","Stylopharyngeus."],["Main motor nerve to most pharyngeal muscles?","Vagus via the pharyngeal plexus."]],"seed":{"title":"Pharynx II","minimumModel":"Pharyngeal constrictors propel the bolus, while longitudinal muscles elevate the pharynx. Most motor supply is vagal via pharyngeal plexus, with stylopharyngeus supplied by CN IX; sensory innervation differs by pharyngeal region.","confusions":["constrictors vs longitudinal muscles","IX stylopharyngeus vs X majority","pharynx vs larynx"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Reconstruct constrictor/longitudinal muscle roles and the major motor exceptions.","transferPrompt":"Use dysphagia plus gag/palate findings to reason about IX/X involvement.","visualAnchor":"Posterior pharyngeal wall muscle layers and plexus."}},"T082":{"id":"T082","topic":"Auditory cortex and encoding","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Auditory cortex and encoding as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Auditory pathway ascends from cochlear nuclei with strong bilateral projections through superior olive/lateral lemniscus/inferior colliculus to medial geniculate body and primary auditory cortex.","Frequency is encoded tonotopically from cochlea through central pathways; binaural timing/intensity comparisons aid sound localization.","Primary auditory cortex lies on superior temporal plane; association cortex interprets complex sound and language-related input."],"mental":"Hearing localization depends on comparing the two ears, not just signal strength in one pathway.","imaginePrompt":"Follow a sound/head-motion event through the physical structures until it becomes a CN VIII signal and central representation.","draw":"Draw cochlear nuclei → superior olive → inferior colliculus → MGB → auditory cortex.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: if shown a graph/diagram/scenario, identify the variable or mechanism, then state the causal interpretation.","checks":[["Thalamic auditory relay?","Medial geniculate body."],["Central auditory pathway laterality?","Strongly bilateral above cochlear nuclei."]],"seed":{"title":"Auditory cortex and sound encoding","minimumModel":"Auditory information ascends bilaterally through brainstem relays to medial geniculate and primary auditory cortex in superior temporal region. Frequency, intensity and spatial cues are encoded across distributed pathways rather than by a single relay.","confusions":["peripheral unilateral hearing loss vs central bilateral representation","primary vs association auditory cortex","frequency vs intensity encoding"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Trace cochlear nuclei → superior olive/lateral lemniscus → inferior colliculus → MGN → auditory cortex.","transferPrompt":"Predict why a unilateral cortical lesion usually does not cause complete unilateral deafness.","visualAnchor":"Ascending auditory pathway with bilateral projections."}},"T083":{"id":"T083","topic":"Nose and paranasal sinuses","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Nose and paranasal sinuses and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Nasal cavity warms, humidifies and filters air; olfactory mucosa occupies superior region. General sensation is via V1/V2 branches.","Paranasal sinuses drain into nasal meatuses; maxillary sinus drainage is disadvantaged by a relatively high natural ostium.","Sphenopalatine artery is a major nasal blood supply; Kiesselbach area is a common anterior epistaxis site."],"mental":"Nasal anatomy is best learned as wall + meatus drainage + vessels + nerves.","imaginePrompt":"Close your eyes and rotate the region. Name what is anterior/posterior/medial/lateral, then draw the simplest map that preserves those relationships.","draw":"Draw lateral nasal wall and sinus drainage into meatuses.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"Practical cue: identify the structure/region first, orient it, then give one relationship/attachment/course feature before adding detail.","checks":[["Common anterior epistaxis area?","Kiesselbach/Little area."],["Olfactory mucosa location?","Superior nasal cavity/roof region."]],"seed":{"title":"Nose and paranasal sinuses","minimumModel":"The nasal cavity has septal/lateral walls and conchae that create meatuses receiving sinus/duct drainage. Paranasal sinuses are frontal, maxillary, ethmoidal and sphenoidal; innervation is mainly trigeminal and olfaction is limited to superior specialized mucosa.","confusions":["middle vs inferior meatus","olfactory vs respiratory mucosa","maxillary vs frontal vs sphenoid drainage"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Draw lateral nasal wall and map major sinus drainage sites into meatuses/recess.","transferPrompt":"Predict which sinus anatomy can make drainage difficult or produce referred facial pain.","visualAnchor":"Lateral nasal wall with conchae, meatuses and sinus ostia."}},"T084":{"id":"T084","topic":"Larynx I","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a usable exam model of Larynx I, not a paragraph memory.","teach":["The larynx is a cartilaginous valve between pharynx and trachea. Unpaired thyroid, cricoid and epiglottic cartilages form the central framework, while paired arytenoid, corniculate and cuneiform cartilages complete the mobile posterior apparatus.","True vocal folds attach anteriorly to the inner thyroid cartilage and posteriorly to vocal processes of the arytenoids. Because the arytenoids rotate and glide on the cricoid, they control whether the rima glottidis opens, closes or changes tension.","Posterior cricoarytenoid is the only major abductor of the vocal folds. Bilateral paralysis can therefore threaten the airway. Lateral cricoarytenoid and interarytenoid muscles adduct the folds for phonation and airway protection.","Cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage relative to cricoid, stretching and tensing the vocal folds and raising pitch. Thyroarytenoid shortens/relaxes the folds and helps fine control of phonation.","Motor innervation is mostly recurrent laryngeal nerve. Cricothyroid is the memorable exception, supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. Sensation above the vocal folds is mainly internal laryngeal nerve; below the folds it is recurrent laryngeal.","Clinically, unilateral recurrent-laryngeal injury usually causes hoarseness, while bilateral injury can produce serious airway compromise. External superior-laryngeal injury weakens pitch control rather than causing the same pattern."],"mental":"Laryngeal cartilage framework + intrinsic muscles change vocal-fold position and tension.","imaginePrompt":"Treat each arytenoid like a tiny movable hinge behind the vocal folds. Rotate them outward to open the airway, inward to close for voice, then tighten the folds with cricothyroid to raise pitch.","draw":"Draw thyroid/cricoid/arytenoid cartilages, vocal folds and recurrent/superior laryngeal nerves.","examGrammar":"Predict voice/airway deficit from muscle or nerve lesion.","practicalCue":"On a laryngeal model, identify thyroid and cricoid first, then arytenoids, true vocal folds, rima glottidis and the posterior cricoarytenoid position.","checks":[["Only major abductor of the vocal folds?","Posterior cricoarytenoid."],["Which intrinsic laryngeal muscle is not supplied by recurrent laryngeal nerve?","Cricothyroid."]],"seed":{"title":"Larynx I","minimumModel":"The laryngeal framework includes thyroid, cricoid, epiglottic and paired arytenoid/corniculate/cuneiform cartilages. Intrinsic muscles move vocal folds and arytenoids; posterior cricoarytenoid is the key abductor.","confusions":["true vs vestibular folds","abductor vs adductor muscles","thyroid vs cricoid cartilage"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Build cartilage framework, vocal-fold attachments and major intrinsic-muscle actions.","transferPrompt":"Predict the airway consequence of bilateral failure of the only major vocal-fold abductor.","visualAnchor":"Laryngeal cartilage framework and vocal-fold movement."}},"T085":{"id":"T085","topic":"Smell","subject":"PHYSIOLOGY","objective":"Explain Smell as a causal mechanism, predict what changes when one link is altered, and retrieve it closed-book.","teach":["Olfactory receptor neurons are bipolar neurons in the olfactory epithelium high in the nasal cavity. Odorant molecules dissolve in mucus and bind receptors on their cilia, initiating a G-protein-mediated transduction cascade that depolarizes the receptor neuron.","Their unmyelinated axons form many small olfactory nerve bundles that pass through the cribriform plate and enter the olfactory bulb. There they converge in glomeruli and synapse mainly on mitral and tufted cells.","Mitral and tufted cell axons form the olfactory tract and project to primary olfactory areas, including piriform/related limbic regions. Olfaction is unusual because primary cortical access does not require an obligatory first thalamic relay.","Olfactory connections to amygdala, hypothalamus and memory-related limbic circuits explain why odors can trigger emotion, autonomic responses and vivid memories.","Adaptation occurs both peripherally and centrally, so a constant odor becomes less noticeable with time even though it may still be present.","A fracture involving the cribriform plate can shear olfactory axons, causing anosmia and potentially allowing CSF leakage through the nose."],"mental":"Olfaction is the sensory exception that initially bypasses thalamus.","imaginePrompt":"Imagine odor molecules landing on cilia at the roof of the nose, then follow the signal through tiny cribriform foramina into glomeruli, mitral cells, olfactory tract and limbic cortex.","draw":"Draw epithelium → cribriform → bulb → tract → primary olfactory cortex.","examGrammar":"Expect mechanism, compare, pathway/lesion or cause→effect discrimination. Answer as an ordered causal chain.","practicalCue":"On a skull-base or nasal diagram, locate olfactory epithelium, cribriform plate, bulb and tract, then connect a fracture site to anosmia/CSF rhinorrhea.","checks":[["Do primary olfactory signals require thalamus before cortex?","No."],["Olfactory axons cross what skull structure?","Cribriform plate."]],"seed":null},"T086":{"id":"T086","topic":"Larynx II, cervical trachea and esophagus","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Larynx I-II cervical trachea esophagus and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid. The external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve supplies cricothyroid. That single exception solves a large fraction of laryngeal nerve questions.","Sensory innervation is divided by the vocal folds: the internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve carries sensation above the folds, while recurrent laryngeal nerve carries sensation below them.","The cervical trachea begins below the cricoid cartilage and descends anterior to the esophagus. Its cartilaginous rings are incomplete posteriorly, where the membranous wall lies against the esophagus.","The thyroid gland lies anterolateral to the upper trachea, and its isthmus crosses the anterior tracheal rings. This relationship matters during tracheostomy and thyroid surgery.","The recurrent laryngeal nerves ascend in or near the tracheoesophageal grooves. They are therefore vulnerable during thyroid operations, especially where they approach the inferior thyroid artery and enter the larynx.","The cervical esophagus begins at the lower border of the cricoid behind the trachea. Dysphagia, hoarseness and airway findings can therefore be anatomically linked by the close relationship of esophagus, trachea, thyroid and recurrent laryngeal nerves."],"mental":"Larynx exception: cricothyroid = external superior laryngeal; everything else intrinsic = recurrent laryngeal.","imaginePrompt":"Stack the neck from front to back: skin/strap muscles → thyroid/trachea → esophagus. Now place the recurrent laryngeal nerves climbing in the grooves between trachea and esophagus.","draw":"Draw laryngeal nerves and vocal fold level.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"On a neck specimen, identify trachea and esophagus first, then trace thyroid lobes/isthmus and look for recurrent laryngeal nerve in the tracheoesophageal groove.","checks":[["Cricothyroid innervation?","External branch superior laryngeal nerve."],["Main motor nerve to other intrinsic laryngeal muscles?","Recurrent laryngeal nerve."]],"seed":null},"T087":{"id":"T087","topic":"Lymph drainage and joints of head and neck","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Lymph drainage and joints head neck and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Head and neck lymph drainage is organized as regional superficial groups feeding the deep cervical chain along the internal jugular vein. The deep cervical nodes are therefore the major final nodal pathway before lymph reaches the jugular lymphatic trunks.","Submental nodes commonly receive central lower lip, chin, floor of mouth and tip of tongue; submandibular nodes receive much of cheek, lateral nose, gums and large parts of anterior oral cavity. Parotid, mastoid and occipital groups receive more lateral/posterior scalp and face territories.","A useful exam strategy is to start from the infected/tumor site, ask which regional superficial group is anatomically nearest, then follow drainage to deep cervical nodes rather than memorizing every site as an isolated pair.","The atlanto-occipital joints between occipital condyles and atlas mainly allow flexion/extension, the 'yes' movement. The median and lateral atlanto-axial joints permit much of head rotation, the 'no' movement.","TMJ combines rotation and translation. Early mouth opening is dominated by rotation in the lower compartment; wider opening adds anterior translation of the mandibular condyle-disc complex in the upper compartment.","Joint questions become easier when reduced to articular surfaces, movement axis, major stabilizers and the movement that would be lost if a structure fails."],"mental":"Yes = atlanto-occipital; no = atlanto-axial.","imaginePrompt":"Trace lymph from a small lesion on the lower lip into a regional node and then down the deep cervical chain. Then switch scenes and nod 'yes', rotate 'no', and open the jaw while feeling the different joint mechanics.","draw":"Draw superficial lymph groups draining to deep cervical chain plus C0-C1/C1-C2 motions.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"On a neck model, identify deep cervical nodes along the IJV; on bone/joint specimens, identify occipital condyles, atlas, dens and TMJ surfaces before naming movements.","checks":[["Major final nodal chain for head/neck?","Deep cervical nodes."],["Primary head rotation joint?","Atlanto-axial."]],"seed":{"title":"Lymph drainage and joints of head and neck","minimumModel":"Head and neck lymph generally drains through regional superficial groups toward deep cervical nodes along the IJV. Key synovial joints include TMJ and upper cervical joints, each with characteristic movements and stabilizing structures.","confusions":["submental vs submandibular drainage","superficial vs deep cervical nodes","atlanto-occipital vs atlanto-axial movement"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map major regional lymph groups to deep cervical chain and contrast TMJ with atlanto-occipital/atlanto-axial movement.","transferPrompt":"Predict likely nodal region from a named oral/pharyngeal site without memorizing every node as an isolated fact.","visualAnchor":"Head/neck lymph map plus upper-cervical joint motion axes."}},"T088":{"id":"T088","topic":"Development of the pharyngeal apparatus","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Development pharyngeal apparatus and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["Each pharyngeal arch contains a characteristic cartilage, muscle group, artery and cranial nerve. The nerve map is the backbone: first arch → CN V, second → VII, third → IX, fourth and sixth → X.","First-arch muscles are mainly muscles of mastication plus mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani and tensor veli palatini. Second-arch muscles include facial-expression muscles, stapedius, stylohyoid and posterior digastric. Third-arch muscle is stylopharyngeus.","Fourth/sixth arches contribute pharyngeal and laryngeal musculature. Superior laryngeal branches are classically linked with fourth-arch derivatives, while recurrent laryngeal branches are linked with sixth-arch derivatives.","Pouches are endodermal internal structures; clefts are ectodermal external structures. The first pouch contributes auditory tube and middle-ear cavity, while the first cleft forms the external acoustic meatus.","The second pouch contributes palatine tonsillar epithelium; third pouch gives thymus and inferior parathyroids; fourth pouch contributes superior parathyroids. These derivative maps are high-yield because they connect embryology to adult neck anatomy.","Persistence of cleft/cervical-sinus tissue can produce branchial cysts or fistulae. The clinical location can therefore be explained by failed obliteration rather than memorized as a random neck lump."],"mental":"Arch nerve map is the skeleton: 1 V, 2 VII, 3 IX, 4/6 X.","imaginePrompt":"Picture five color-coded arches stacked around the embryonic pharynx. Give each one its nerve first, then hang its muscles and skeletal derivatives from that nerve like labeled keys.","draw":"Create arch 1/2/3/4/6 table: nerve, muscle theme, skeletal derivative.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"On an embryology diagram, separate arch, pouch and cleft before naming derivatives. On a neck cyst vignette, connect location to persistence of the cervical sinus/cleft system.","checks":[["Nerve of second arch?","Facial nerve."],["First cleft derivative?","External acoustic meatus."]],"seed":{"title":"Development of pharyngeal apparatus","minimumModel":"Pharyngeal arches appear in early craniofacial development; each arch carries characteristic cartilage, muscle, arterial and cranial-nerve components, while pouches and clefts have distinct endodermal versus ectodermal derivatives.","confusions":["arch vs pouch vs cleft","first vs second arch derivatives","third vs fourth/sixth arch patterns"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Build a compact arch table organized by nerve → muscles → skeletal derivative, then separate pouch from cleft derivatives.","transferPrompt":"Infer the likely arch involved when a developmental defect combines a characteristic muscle and cranial-nerve pattern.","visualAnchor":"Color-coded pharyngeal arches, pouches and clefts."}},"T089":{"id":"T089","topic":"Development of face, palate and tongue","subject":"ANATOMY","objective":"Build a manipulable 3D map of Development face palate and tongue and reproduce the high-yield relations without reopening notes.","teach":["The face develops around the primitive mouth from the frontonasal prominence plus paired maxillary and mandibular prominences. Medial and lateral nasal prominences form within the frontonasal region as the nasal placodes invaginate.","The upper lip is assembled mainly from fused maxillary prominences and the intermaxillary segment derived from medial nasal prominences. Failure of fusion between maxillary and medial nasal components can produce cleft lip.","The primary palate comes from the intermaxillary segment. The secondary palate develops mainly from paired palatal shelves of the maxillary prominences, which elevate and fuse with each other and with the nasal septum. Failure gives cleft-palate patterns.","The anterior two-thirds of the tongue is mainly first-arch territory for general sensation through lingual nerve, V3, while taste is carried largely by chorda tympani, VII. The posterior third is mainly third-arch territory and therefore linked to IX.","The epiglottic/posterior-most tongue region is linked with fourth arch and vagal sensation. Tongue muscles themselves arise largely from migrating occipital myotomes, explaining motor supply by hypoglossal nerve except palatoglossus.","Mixed tongue innervation is not arbitrary. It is an embryology fossil: different mucosal regions retain nerves associated with the arches that formed them."],"mental":"Mixed tongue innervation is an embryology fingerprint.","imaginePrompt":"Run a time-lapse of the face closing around the primitive mouth, then lift two palatal shelves horizontally until they meet. Finish by coloring the tongue according to arch origin and nerve supply.","draw":"Draw facial prominences and palate fusion; then map tongue thirds to arch/nerve.","examGrammar":"Expect identify/relations/boundaries/course/branches/applied-anatomy prompts. Answer in compact marking units, then redraw the relation.","practicalCue":"On an embryology diagram, identify frontonasal/maxillary/mandibular components first, then palatal shelves and tongue regions. Use fusion failure location to infer the defect.","checks":[["Secondary palate mainly arises from?","Palatine shelves of maxillary prominences."],["Why tongue innervation is mixed?","Different regions arise from different pharyngeal arches."]],"seed":{"title":"Development of face, palate and tongue","minimumModel":"The face forms by fusion/growth of frontonasal, maxillary and mandibular prominences; primary and secondary palate formation requires coordinated fusion of facial/palatal processes. The tongue has contributions from multiple pharyngeal arches, explaining its mixed sensory and motor innervation.","confusions":["primary vs secondary palate","maxillary vs medial nasal processes","anterior vs posterior tongue arch contributions"],"closedGenerationPrompt":"Map major facial prominences into upper/lower face, outline palate fusion, then connect tongue regions to arch-derived innervation.","transferPrompt":"Use a cleft location or tongue sensory pattern to infer which developmental process/arch relationship is relevant.","visualAnchor":"Embryonic facial prominences plus palate fusion and tongue arch map."}}};